WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:05.780
OK.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.420
So let's get started.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.887
Today is the second and last
of the scaling laws lectures.

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:14.720
And today it's going
to be a little bit more

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.900
of a case study and
details-oriented lecture.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.220
I'm going to cover
two separate things.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.050
The first one is I'm going to
go through a couple of papers

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.140
where people have done careful
scaling law studies as part

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.790
of their model building.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:32.000
And I'm going to use that as
a way to convey to you how

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.590
modern language model builders
use scaling laws as part

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.090
of their design process.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.030
So motivation from
last time and today

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.990
is, what's the best practice
for scaling a large model.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:49.460
We want to have large language
models with nice hyperparameters

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.500
and good architecture choices.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.660
And I've already told
you about Chinchilla,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.250
and using scaling laws
to validate some of this.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.530
But I think you should have
rightfully skeptical questions

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.580
about scaling laws.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.780
It's curve fitting
on a log, log plot,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.820
is it really as good as I
said it was last lecture?

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.000
So does Chinchilla's approach
to scaling laws actually work?

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.480
You're finding this out
in your assignments.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.520
If you fit an isoflop,
is that really

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.400
telling you about the
right token trade-offs?

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.750
Can you use this stuff to really
set optimal learning rates?

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.300
And should we be picking
particular architectures

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.290
or parameterizations
to scale nicely?

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.650
So the last paper
or the newest paper

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.620
we talked about with
lots of detailed scaling

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.950
studies in last lecture was
the DeepMind Chinchilla paper.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.700
And after that,
ChatGPT happened.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.200
And the competitive landscape
of large language model building

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.080
really changed.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.900
And people just stopped
publishing anything

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.000
about data, and scaling,
and all these things.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.900
It was very secretive.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.270
I've talked to people at some
of the frontier labs before

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.040
and ask them oh, what are
you guys doing for scaling.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.290
And they're like, no, you
will not tell you anything

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:04.498
about what we do for scaling.

00:02:04.498 --> 00:02:08.720
And so we have to rely
on other sources for how

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.199
scaling happens in practice.

00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:14.810
And there have been several
competently executed large scale

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.570
models that have done scaling.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.000
And so last year
in this lecture,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.550
I covered Cerebras-GPT,
DeepSeek LLM, and MiniCPM.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.500
And as a nice side
note, last year,

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.360
I had to really
strongly justify why

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.488
I was covering these
Chinese models, so to speak.

00:02:32.488 --> 00:02:34.280
But this year, thankfully,
hopefully you're

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.680
all already excited to hear
about DeepSeek rather than me

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.130
trying to convince you that this
is the right thing to listen to.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.290
In the years since
then, I've looked

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.470
at a lot of the models
that have come out.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:49.070
Actually the hall in terms
of new scaling law insights

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:52.070
and papers is
actually much sparser.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.630
I'll briefly
mention some results

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:58.240
from Llama 3, which came out
at the later end of last year.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.970
Hunyuan-Large, which is
a MoE model from China,

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:06.310
and then MiniMax 01, which is
a linear time hybrid attention

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:10.500
model, or long context model
that came out this year.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.000
And all three of those have
some scaling studies, but really

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.240
nothing quite as extensive
as DeepSeek or MiniCPM which

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.650
have really been the
gold standard, I think,

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:21.970
for modern scaling law studies.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.830
So that's one part of what
I want to talk about today.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.700
I want to make sure you guys
have an understanding of what

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.912
scaling looks like in a
real semi-production model.

00:03:31.912 --> 00:03:33.620
And the other thing
I want to talk about,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.850
which is an important
deep dive, I think,

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.140
is the mu P method that
I mentioned last time.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:46.690
So mu P is this approach, just
as a recap of last lecture,

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.540
is when we train these models,
as we make them bigger,

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.283
we need to change
certain hyperparameters.

00:03:53.283 --> 00:03:54.700
On the left hand
side of this plot

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.630
here, you see that as you make
models wider, in this case,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.250
like an MLP, you
make them wider,

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.400
the optimum learning
rate shifts downwards.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.960
So you need smaller learning
rates for these bigger models.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.230
And that's a really big problem
potentially because then you

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:11.750
need to hyperparameter tune
your learning rates at the very

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.660
large scale, and that's going
to be very computationally

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:15.240
expensive.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.089
It's going to be a huge problem.

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.600
On the other hand, if we
could parameterize our model

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.750
differently so that the
learning rate that's optimal

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.600
just stayed the same forever
across all the scales,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.720
that's great.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.290
That's really simplified
our search process.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.810
We would like all of our
hyperparameters and really

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.210
choices, in general, to
remain stable across scales.

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.260
That's the ideal.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.290
And mu P is a very interesting
class of approaches.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.960
And it teaches us some
pretty interesting ways

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.210
of thinking about the problem.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.877
So I'm going to
actually go through some

00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:48.150
of the details in the math.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.400
In the years since--
last time I taught this,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.325
there were a couple of very nice
tutorials on mu P that came out,

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:55.450
so I'm going to follow
those because they have math

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.170
that's pretty easy to follow.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:59.680
And then I'll talk
about some work that

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.930
has come out doing third party
validation and evaluation of mu

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.970
P style methods.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:08.500
OK.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.700
The focus of the first
part of this lecture,

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.340
which is the case study, is
going to be on three models.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.950
I talked about three
additional more modern models,

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.890
but actually the details in
those are much more sparse.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.460
And I think the lessons
you learn are primarily

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.210
from these three papers here.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.483
So that's going to be my
focus for the first part

00:05:25.483 --> 00:05:26.150
of this lecture.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.900
So I'm going to talk about
Cerebras-GPT, MiniCPM,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:29.660
and DeepSeek.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.290
And each one of these will have
actually a pretty different mix

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:33.970
of scaling strategies,
and it'll also

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:35.440
have different
things to teach us

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.560
about how to get scaling right.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.170
So we'll get started.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:46.480
Cerebras-GPT is the first of
the models and scaling things

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.490
that I want to talk about.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.540
It's a large family of models.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.220
It's trained 0.1 to 13 billion
parameter models, trained

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:58.310
with the Chinchilla recipe,
so roughly the same number

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:02.090
of token to parameter
counts as is optimal.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:05.810
And they have an
interesting core finding.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.850
The Cerebras folks
actually are pretty

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.790
interested in a lot of these
scaling and parameterization

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:11.640
studies.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.400
And they have a really
interesting core finding,

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.700
which is they scale up this mu
P thing that I mentioned before,

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.700
and they find that
it makes scaling

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.230
a lot more stable and a lot
more pleasant to deal with.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.960
And just to show
you the punchline,

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.690
you've got test
loss on the pile,

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.880
and you've got the
scaling curves here

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.110
of the Cerebras-GPT in blue.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.940
This is with standard
parameterization.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.070
You've got mu P in orange.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.310
This is the model
that they also train

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.540
with using the maximal
update parameterization.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.960
And they show that it
scales more nicely,

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:48.800
if not better, than things like
Pythia or GPT-J. So that's nice.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.000
And the thing that I
want to emphasize here

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.010
is that this is one of the few,
if not first, public validations

00:06:57.010 --> 00:07:01.600
of mu P. We know that all of or
most of the labs that are doing

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.510
LLM scaling pay close attention
to how they parameterize

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.860
their networks, their
initializations,

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:08.810
as a function of the
scale of the model,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.080
as well as things like
per layer learning rates

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:14.080
are things that people pay close
attention to make scaling much

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.800
more stable.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:19.765
And so things like mu P are
pretty important in this space.

00:07:19.765 --> 00:07:22.930
104, for example, the
paper for that isn't out

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:24.740
and I don't know
if it will be out,

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.390
but they talk about a technique
they call meta P, which

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:31.060
is a variant of this as well.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.180
So what they show
is that when they

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.710
train models using
standard parameterization,

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:40.450
they find that they
have big oscillations

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.170
around the predicted
scaling point.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:43.730
So that's this dashed line.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:45.640
They have oscillations
due to the fact

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.765
that, for example, they
have to adjust the learning

00:07:47.765 --> 00:07:49.630
rate as a function of scale.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.190
And so it's hard for
them to really get

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.720
the predicted performance
exactly right,

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.690
which is this dashed line
using their scaling recipe.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:59.660
On the other hand,
what they find

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:06.480
is if you have the three risk
GPT, sorry, mu P scaling,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.520
then you get this orange
line, which is much,

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.320
much closer to the scaling
law fit for this mu P version.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.030
And so their claim
here, at least,

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.270
is that using this
alternative parameterization

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.210
allows them to get much more
predictable scaling, much more

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.070
nice hyperparameter tuning.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:25.040
We're going to see
this in more detail.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:26.810
I'll return to this
slide again once I've

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:30.200
gone through the mathematical
derivation of mu P.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.450
But in case you're ever
interested in implementing

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:35.039
this thing, some of
the Cerebras-GPT folks,

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:40.130
and in general, the artifacts
that the serious research

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:42.950
folks are putting out is
very, very helpful for mu P

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.830
because they have this big table
in the appendix that really just

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:48.730
tells you exactly the
difference between the standard

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:50.650
initialization and
parameterization or SP

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.590
and the maximum update
version or mu P.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.940
And you'll see that--

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.350
I'll just give you
the one liner version.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.080
Basically, every
nonembedding parameter

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.190
is initialized with
1 over the width.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:03.040
And then the learning
rates per layer

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:06.380
are scaled down by
1 over the width

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:09.590
So the interesting difference
from standard parameterization,

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:11.890
even if you're already
doing 1 over width

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.900
scaling on the
initialization, is actually

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.400
there's per layer learning
rates that are different.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.380
And I'm going to
get to that later.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.500
I'm going to do a full
derivation of this result.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.455
But you can see here this
nice quick reference.

00:09:24.455 --> 00:09:26.330
And also if you want to
implement this thing,

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:32.020
this gives you very easy
ways of implementing mu P.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:33.610
Another interesting
thing that we also

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:36.340
see in some of the
other scaling strategies

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.560
is that you combine
these strategies

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.090
like mu P which
makes hyperparameter

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:43.420
selection stable with very,
very aggressive scaling.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:46.750
So what they do here
is they scale down

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.950
their experiments all the way
down to 40 million parameters.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:54.570
They do extensive hyperparameter
search on this proxy model.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.680
And then they scale
things back up

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.340
using mu P to try to
keep hyperparameters

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.620
as stable as possible.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:06.080
And so this is what they see in
their small scale hyperparameter

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:06.580
search.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.290
Each one of these
dots is a model run.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.122
And there's a
hyperparameter associated

00:10:10.122 --> 00:10:11.080
with each one of these.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:13.390
And then they pick the
minimum across these runs,

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:17.790
giving them essentially
their hyperparameter grid.

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:21.310
This is a very clean approach
to hyperparameter selection.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:24.313
It's unclear whether this level
of aggressive scaling down

00:10:24.313 --> 00:10:26.980
is really what you want to do if
you want to train these really,

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:28.470
really large models.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:31.140
But this is a one
strategy that we see also

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.273
in many CPM and
DeepSeek, like, training

00:10:33.273 --> 00:10:35.190
much smaller surrogate
models, and then trying

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:37.780
to figure out how to
stably scale them back up.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:41.850
And that's going to be a
theme that we see throughout.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.483
And yeah, if folks have
questions, please stop me.

00:10:44.483 --> 00:10:46.900
Actually, maybe I'll stop here
for a moment in case anyone

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:50.387
has questions for this
Cerebras-GPT piece,

00:10:50.387 --> 00:10:51.970
although maybe it'll
be clearer once I

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:54.780
talk about the derivation
later in this lecture.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:02.530
There's another paper I
want to talk about, MiniCPM,

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:06.040
or another artifact, I guess.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.470
And for whatever
reason, I think MiniCPM

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.230
hasn't been talked
about quite as much,

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.770
especially in Western
academic circles.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:19.720
But at least for me, this
was one of the first releases

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.930
or papers I saw coming out of
a Chinese research group where

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:26.740
they had done some really
cool in-depth scaling

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:27.860
and other research.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:31.292
It really felt like stuff
coming out of the frontier.

00:11:31.292 --> 00:11:33.710
And to give you an
overview of what they do,

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:37.420
their goals here is they want to
train relatively small language

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.610
models, but use a lot of
compute to train really

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:40.880
good small language models.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:42.490
That's their ostensible goal.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:46.330
And in doing so, they do a lot
of careful scaling computations.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:50.460
They also once again use mu P to
stabilize and simplify scaling

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.730
when they end up scaling these
models not in size, but in terms

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.220
of the amount of data.

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:01.290
And to try to convince you that
this is a paper worth following,

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.310
at the time that
they were trained,

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:08.520
this was a remarkably
good 1.2 to 2.4B models.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:12.420
It beats out most of the 2B
models that were out there,

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:15.810
and it matched many of
the modern 7B models,

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.970
at least modern as
of 2024 standards.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.370
I mean, now, of course, you've
got even better 7B models.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.130
The arms race is fierce.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.650
But this should give you a
sense that at least given

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:27.390
the amount of compute
and technology

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:30.420
available back in
mid-2024, this was actually

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:31.840
really at the frontier.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:36.030
And they did something right
to get models of this quality.

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:40.890
And so much like Cerebras-GPT,
essentially, they

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:44.452
have to have some strategy
to get scaling right.

00:12:44.452 --> 00:12:45.910
So stepping back
right you're going

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:47.295
to do a really big model run.

00:12:47.295 --> 00:12:49.670
What do you have to do, you
have to pick hyperparameters.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.310
You have to make sure those
hyperparameters scale nicely,

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.246
and then you scale
up your model.

00:12:54.246 --> 00:12:58.070
So we can do the same thing
as the Cerebras-GPT folks.

00:12:58.070 --> 00:12:59.830
We can try to pick
hyperparameters

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:02.710
at a small scale, hope
that they stay stable,

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:04.040
and then scale everything up.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.800
And the way to do that would
be to use something like mu P.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.140
And this has exactly the same
strategy at play here, you see.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.160
So for embedding, you
don't really do anything,

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.100
you just scale it by a constant.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:18.560
Whenever you have some of
residual connection like an MLP,

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.940
you scale it by the square
root of the number of layers.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:26.780
You initialize it by
1 over the base width.

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:29.980
And then, the learning
rates are also

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.090
scaled by the
width of the model.

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:36.100
And we see basically the same
strategy or the same scaling

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.860
factors appear as the
Cerebras-GPT case.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:44.040
And they also end up with
very similar parameters

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:48.210
as Cerebras-GPT, the
same scale embeddings,

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:51.970
similar learning rates off
by a factor of two or so.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.480
But generally, you end
up in similar places

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.600
as these hyperparameters.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.840
And then what you do
is once you have this,

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:04.478
you're relying on your optimum
learning rates to remain stable.

00:14:04.478 --> 00:14:06.520
So you're just going to
keep those roughly fixed.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:09.862
And we know that the aspect
ratio is a pretty important

00:14:09.862 --> 00:14:11.820
thing, so we just fixed
that after figuring out

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:12.760
what the right one is.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:14.550
And then you scale
up the overall model

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.310
size going all the
way from 9 or 30

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:21.280
M, all the way to 0.5 or one
billion parameter models.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:25.830
And so what they have is a
roughly 5x or maybe a little bit

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:28.530
more compute savings going
from the smallest models that

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:32.605
they've got to the largest
pilot run models that they have.

00:14:32.605 --> 00:14:34.230
And now, you can use
this, and then you

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:38.850
can figure out whether
you have optimal batch

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:40.880
sizes as a function of scale.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.390
So you want to figure
out B crit, which

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:44.780
is the critical batch size.

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:46.870
If you remember
correctly, this is--

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:49.440
the critical batch size is
roughly the diminishing returns

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:49.940
point.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.820
So as models get bigger,
their losses get lower.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:54.460
As their loss gets
lower, you can

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.420
make use of bigger and
bigger batch sizes.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.270
So the critical
batch size is roughly

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:00.820
telling you for the given
model size and scale

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:04.150
that I'm operating at, what is
the appropriate global batch

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.310
size for me to be training
these models with.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:10.070
And so much like
the Kaplan paper,

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:11.720
they follow a similar recipe.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.820
The plots look different
from the Kaplan paper,

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.615
but the underlying
strategy is the same.

00:15:15.615 --> 00:15:16.990
What they're trying
to figure out

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:19.613
is what is the critical
batch size for training

00:15:19.613 --> 00:15:21.280
or the optimal batch
size, in this case,

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:23.030
for training different models.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.810
And they're trying to find
relatively predictable scaling

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:28.630
relationships between
the batch size,

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:32.440
and, for example, the data
size, or the loss size.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:35.440
And vertical columns here
represent a single training

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:35.990
curve.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:39.600
And then the quadratics
are being fitted

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.350
to try to identify the minimum.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:44.490
So the red line
here is the minimum

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.740
across all of these
points as we go upwards.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.830
And this is trying to
tell us the optimum batch

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:55.890
size for a particular choice of
model size and data set size.

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.010
And then at this point, you
can follow the same logic

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.730
as the Kaplan paper for
identifying the batch sizes.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:05.380
Basically, you
reproduce the same plot.

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:08.490
If you remember the Kaplan paper
and the critical batch size

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.290
discussion from
two lectures ago,

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.480
if not can pull up
the lecture slides,

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.490
you'll remember that
basically, the thing that's

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:17.580
highly predictable is the loss
that you're trying to train to,

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:21.060
the terminal loss, and
the batch size of--

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:22.510
the critical batch size point.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.650
And so we see that, once
again, much like in Kaplan,

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:27.930
you see a log
linear relationship

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:32.130
here between the target loss or
the terminal loss and the batch

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:33.070
size that you want.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.392
And so from this,
you can figure out

00:16:35.392 --> 00:16:36.850
what batch size
you're going to get

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:38.833
because if you have a
particular target scale,

00:16:38.833 --> 00:16:40.750
you can use scaling loss
to figure out what is

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:42.110
the loss that I expect to get.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:44.027
Once you know the loss
that you expect to get,

00:16:44.027 --> 00:16:45.610
you can use that
to back out what

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:47.540
batch size you can operate at.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.740
So there's a fairly clean trend,
polynomially increase the batch

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:54.190
size as loss decreases.

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:59.852
Now, batch sizes do shift around
as a function of target loss

00:16:59.852 --> 00:17:00.560
and thus compute.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:02.750
So we have to fit a
scaling law for that guy.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:07.210
But we already did mu P. And so
in theory, if the approach works

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.670
at all, what we
should now get is

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:13.990
we should get that the optimum
learning rate here is stable.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:17.050
So on this plot, we're seeing
essentially different model

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:19.960
sizes from small models
in the light colors

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.089
to their biggest models
in the dark colors.

00:17:23.089 --> 00:17:25.369
And you see them varying
different learning rates.

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:27.369
The big models are only
running for a little bit

00:17:27.369 --> 00:17:28.930
for compute reasons.

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:31.250
But what you see is
a fairly clear trend.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:34.930
And once again, very consistent
with some of the earlier results

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:38.280
that we've seen in Kaplan et
al where you have a relatively

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:41.460
wide basin and then sharp
increases as your model

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:42.845
becomes very unstable.

00:17:42.845 --> 00:17:44.970
But the important thing
here is that the minimum

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:48.630
remains fixed across relatively
large orders of magnitude.

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:50.800
From your small model
to the big model,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:52.960
the minimum, or at least
tied with the minimum,

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:55.920
is at the exact same
point at roughly 10

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.360
to the negative 2 learning rate.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.050
And so this is a nice
piece of evidence

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:05.880
or some validation that
properly scaling your model

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.490
initialization and properly
scaling your per layer learning

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:13.570
rates allow you to avoid tuning
learning rates over and over,

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:16.140
or even fitting scaling
laws on learning rates

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.805
in order to try to predict what
the optimal learning rate is.

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:25.560
And then the final
thing is you might

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.990
want to figure out essentially
model size to data trade-offs.

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:31.200
If you're training
small models, you're

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.400
going to be probably
overtraining your models,

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:35.390
or at least you want
to justify to yourself

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:37.580
why you're training
on so many tokens,

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:39.950
and so you might want
to replicate something

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:42.140
like the Chinchilla analysis.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:47.270
So the MiniCPM people had a
really cool or nice innovation.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:48.890
Others have done
similar things, but I

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:50.807
think they were the first
to really popularize

00:18:50.807 --> 00:18:52.970
this in the LLM
setting, especially

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:57.900
in the context of Chinchilla
style scaling is the following.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:00.650
So let's say I want to fit
a Chinchilla scaling law.

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:02.610
When I do that, what
do I need to do?

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:06.450
Well, I need to vary
the number of tokens,

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:08.530
and I need to vary model sizes.

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.172
And so when I do that, I'm
going to fix a model size,

00:19:11.172 --> 00:19:13.380
and I'm going to train a
model for longer and longer.

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.650
It would be nice if I could
of early stop and take

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:17.630
the checkpoints of
this model, and have

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:20.390
that be the difference or
changes to the data set size.

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.290
Because earlier
checkpoints see less data,

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.940
it would be nice if I could
use a single run to collect all

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:27.810
of this data scaling things.

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:29.600
Unfortunately, what
I'm showing here

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:33.110
is that the cosine learning
rates for different data targets

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:33.750
are different.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:35.800
So if you have a very
small amount of data,

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:38.190
you have a cosine that goes
up very quickly or sorry,

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:42.120
that goes up, the warmup is
always the same, but a very fast

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:42.790
cool down.

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:44.710
You train for a little bit and
then you come down very quickly.

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:46.293
If you have a lot
of data, then you're

00:19:46.293 --> 00:19:48.250
going to very slowly
come down to the end.

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:50.897
And so your learning rates
between a small data training

00:19:50.897 --> 00:19:52.980
run and a big data training
run will be different.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.097
This is a very, very key point.

00:19:55.097 --> 00:19:56.680
Lots of people get
tripped up by this.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:00.510
You cannot use a single run of
a cosine learning rate model

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:03.900
and try to get early checkpoints
and reason about data scaling

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:04.990
behavior based on that.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:07.240
This bites people all the time.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:10.050
And so in order to avoid
this, what you would normally

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:12.990
need to do is you need to
train a model from start

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:14.650
to every single endpoint.

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:16.870
So you have to train it
to every single target.

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:20.082
And so this takes you
to n squared runs.

00:20:20.082 --> 00:20:21.540
Some of the runs
are small, but you

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:24.660
have to basically run lots of
runs, each one with a target

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:26.760
termination point rather
than using a single run

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.060
and collecting checkpoints.

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:31.640
It feels senseless that
we have to do this.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:38.630
So the MiniCPM folks popularized
this idea of a WSD or warm-up

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:40.700
stable decay learning rate.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.830
And so this plot
on the left really

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:45.570
shows you what's going on here.

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.990
Normally what we would
train with is something

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:50.120
that looks like
this cosine learning

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:51.690
rate shown in yellow here.

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:52.590
It goes up.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:54.740
There's a warm
period, usually very

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:57.090
short to get to your
full learning rate.

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:59.450
And then there's a cosine
that goes all the way down

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:01.430
to a termination
point, and maybe you

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:03.090
stay at your minimum
learning rate.

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:04.500
This is all, of
course, optional.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:05.970
You might terminate
here as well.

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:08.090
You might go all the way to 0.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:09.960
And so cosine learning
rate looks like this.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:11.030
And the issue
here, of course, is

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:12.320
that if I have a
different target,

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:14.153
the cosine is going to
be totally different.

00:21:14.153 --> 00:21:17.330
So everything past the
warmup can't be reused.

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:20.870
Now, if you look at this
new WSD, which is basically

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:23.790
a trapezoid learning rate,
what it has is three phases.

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:26.400
It's got a warmup phase
that's the same as the cosine,

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:28.320
it's got a stable
phase that's flat,

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:30.450
and then it's got
a decay phase that

00:21:30.450 --> 00:21:33.170
rapidly cools down the model
down to its minimum learning

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:33.670
rate.

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:35.420
And of course, you can
variations of this.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:38.130
You can go up, down, and then
stay stable at your minimum.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:39.790
You can do any of
these variations.

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:42.480
But I think in general,
the simplest form

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:46.291
to think about is warmup,
stable, decay, terminate.

00:21:46.291 --> 00:21:47.710
Why is this nice?

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:51.010
This is nice because you
can reuse the stable part.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:52.860
So the thing that
you do is if you

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:55.470
want to do Chinchilla
in almost one run, what

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:58.620
you do is you warmup,
you have a stable run

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:01.060
all the way to the end,
and then you cool down.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:03.060
And then if you want to
figure out oh, how would

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:04.960
my model have been
if I used less data,

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:08.410
you rewind the checkpoints
and then you do another down.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:10.680
And now you've got an
exact warmup stable decay

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:13.468
learning rate shape without
having done the training

00:22:13.468 --> 00:22:14.260
from the beginning.

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:15.658
So this is a very nice thing.

00:22:15.658 --> 00:22:17.700
The fact that the stable
part essentially is flat

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.870
allows you to do Chinchilla
style scaling or data scaling

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:24.510
in a single training run,
or for mostly the cost

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:25.770
of a single training run.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.110
And a lot of people now do this.

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:29.930
OK.

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:32.090
So they work very well.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.350
MiniCPM, I think,
popularized this.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:37.080
And I think a lot of people
have since then adopted it.

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:42.190
And we see a lot of WSD style
schedules in many, many places.

00:22:42.190 --> 00:22:44.490
You see curves that look this.

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:46.680
If you have a cosine
learning rate schedule,

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:50.360
you'll see essentially,
relatively predictable

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:52.670
smooth decay towards
your terminal loss

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:54.590
like this yellow line here.

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:58.700
If you train with WSD, you'll
see much, much funkier learning

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:01.790
curves that look like the
curves that I have here

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:03.602
above them, the darker lines.

00:23:03.602 --> 00:23:05.810
So you've got your warmup
phase, which doesn't really

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:07.102
show up in this training curve.

00:23:07.102 --> 00:23:07.710
It's so short.

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:09.085
Then you've got
your stable phase

00:23:09.085 --> 00:23:10.950
where it goes down normally.

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:13.140
And then as soon as you
hit your decay phase,

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:16.310
like the cool down part, your
loss really rapidly drops off

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:19.280
until you've hit your either
0 or minimum learning rate

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:22.410
point, at which point you've
gotten your terminal loss

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:25.890
So these losses may look
very disturbing to you,

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.470
but they are actually
pretty normal when

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:33.430
you're training with these
rapid cool down learning curves.

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:36.700
And maybe the point to make here
is at every single token count,

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:40.530
you see that the warmup stable
decay curve, the minimum point,

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:42.848
beats or matches the
cosine learning rate.

00:23:42.848 --> 00:23:44.140
But that's not always the case.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.380
There can sometimes be
cases where cosine works

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:47.920
better or WSD works better.

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:50.610
But in general, I
think a lot of the,

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:52.560
I think, things
that people say here

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.180
is that the two learning
rates are roughly comparable,

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:57.533
but WSD has the
additional nice advantage

00:23:57.533 --> 00:23:59.950
that you don't have to worry
about your termination point.

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:02.220
You can repeatedly cool
down to get check points

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:03.395
of different data counts.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:08.380
Cool.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.890
And then of course,
there's other things

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:14.280
that have appeared for trying
to estimate Chinchilla curves.

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:17.580
Some folks, a collaboration
of like U-Dub, formerly

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:21.420
U-Dub-- and Apple
folks, had this paper

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.950
on estimating the
Chinchilla penalty.

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:26.990
That is when you keep
adding more and more data,

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:30.320
how much worse is your
loss than if you had

00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:31.830
scaled according to Chinchilla?

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:34.220
So you have your
teal line here, which

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:38.610
is M equals 20
tokens to parameters.

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.550
And you can think
about, OK, what

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:43.440
happens if I train with
320 tokens to parameters.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:45.660
Well, then you have a separate
parallel scaling line.

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:47.202
And then you have
another line, which

00:24:47.202 --> 00:24:50.210
is the circles, which is,
what if I train with 640

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:52.080
or the darker one is there.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:54.710
And so the thing that
they show is actually

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:56.910
instead of doing
this WSB style thing,

00:24:56.910 --> 00:25:00.060
another thing you could do is
you could try to figure out, OK,

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:04.010
how much does my model degrade
as a function of higher

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:05.580
tokens to parameter ratios?

00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:09.030
Well, that turns out also to
have a fairly predictable shape.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.870
And you can extrapolate
that based on degradation

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:13.340
at small training runs.

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:15.290
I don't think I've
seen large scale

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:16.830
training runs using this idea.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:18.440
But it's an
additional cool thing

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:22.490
to know that essentially could
do Chinchilla in almost one

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:26.850
training run by extrapolating
the access token penalty

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:29.640
at a small scale as well.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:32.370
So OK, going back to MiniCPM.

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:34.690
Now, we have the
tools that we need.

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:36.240
We have the WSB
learning rate, which

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.380
allows us to essentially
do one training run.

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:42.430
And that one training run allows
us to have both variations.

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:44.370
Sorry, that one
training run allows

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.225
us to vary data as we go along.

00:25:46.225 --> 00:25:47.850
And then we have
multiple training runs

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:50.340
for different model sizes,
that gives us all that we

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:52.950
need to do Chinchilla analysis.

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.208
And they use method
1 and method 3,

00:25:55.208 --> 00:25:56.500
if you remember what those are.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.037
Method 1 is you overlay
all of the learning curves,

00:25:59.037 --> 00:26:00.370
and you take the lower envelope.

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:02.700
And the lower envelope of
all the training curves

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:06.340
is supposed to be
roughly a power law.

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:08.730
And then method 3 is
you basically jointly

00:26:08.730 --> 00:26:10.500
fit this equation
2 you have here,

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:14.260
you hypothesize this two
variable scaling law,

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:16.650
and you fit it to all
the data that you have

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:18.990
and curve fitting style fashion.

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:23.160
And then that allows you to
solve for the optimum token

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:26.810
to data ratio through that fit.

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:28.550
So they do both of that.

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:31.890
They do see for Chinchilla
method 1, fairly clear,

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:33.920
although not perfectly
linear trends

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:41.090
that allow them to essentially
go from compute to token ratios.

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:43.520
And their primary
approach that they

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:45.890
use to justify a lot of
their design decisions

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:46.830
is the method 3.

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:47.820
It's the curve fitting.

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:51.150
So the contours that you see
here is the curve that they fit.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.840
The dots that they have
here is the small scale

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:56.750
runs that they did to fit
the Chinchilla parameters.

00:26:56.750 --> 00:27:01.740
And just to justify
what they do,

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:05.120
they find very, very high
token to parameter ratios,

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:08.300
like so high that I feel
like this is an outlier that

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:11.120
doesn't really agree
very closely with most

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:12.860
of the other literature.

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.740
They argue that Llama style
architecture should all

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:18.380
have a higher ratio
because of improved data

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:20.640
quality and improved
model efficiency,

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:22.490
but their token to
parameter ratio estimates

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:23.520
are really, really high.

00:27:23.520 --> 00:27:26.580
192 tokens per parameter,
which I don't think I've

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:29.080
seen anyone else derive.

00:27:29.080 --> 00:27:31.570
I think other people have done
replications of Chinchilla.

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:35.460
I don't think anyone's
ever really done or argued

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.770
for 192 tokens to parameter.

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:41.730
Regardless, we have seen that
recent models like Llama 3

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:44.590
have significantly higher
data to model ratios.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:46.650
We also don't really
see diminishing returns.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:49.860
These models aren't way worse
than the equivalent Chinchilla

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:51.910
scaled like Llama 2 models.

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:55.210
This suggests that with careful
optimization and careful tuning,

00:27:55.210 --> 00:27:57.390
we should be able to go
far beyond the 20 times

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:59.460
model size rule of thumb.

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:01.200
So if there's one
thing you take away

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:04.450
from this set of
these last two slides,

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:06.450
maybe not necessarily
that you should

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:10.230
trust whatever scaling
law fits that MiniCPM did,

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:13.680
but rather that the Chinchilla
analysis isn't really

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:16.080
a strong constraint.

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:18.400
20 times model size is
just a starting point,

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:22.220
you should be feeling free
to significantly increase

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:26.060
that token to parameter ratio.

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:29.360
Finally, the curve fits
that they get are generally

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:30.710
pretty good looking.

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:32.510
So this is the
scaling law curves

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:37.010
for essentially data
and model size scaling

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.330
and perplexities
on code in English.

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:41.870
They do have some really weird
outliers that I don't really

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:44.540
understand why they get these.

00:28:44.540 --> 00:28:48.440
But the fitted scaling laws
are generally pretty good

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:51.050
as they increase the amount
of data on their relatively

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:51.810
small model.

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:56.240
So this is one example of a
large scale training run scaling

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:56.820
recipe.

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:59.270
So I'll stop here.

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:01.920
Things like WSD are
probably new to you.

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:04.280
So if you have any
questions, please

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:06.440
feel free to ask or
any of the other bits

00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:11.590
including like the Chinchilla
replication and mu P and so on.

00:29:11.590 --> 00:29:12.090
Oh, OK.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:13.130
Sure.

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:15.320
[INAUDIBLE] just like
the main adaptation

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:18.680
of [INAUDIBLE] in terms
of initializing the weight

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:20.140
of parameters [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:20.140 --> 00:29:22.165
So the question was
the main change in mu

00:29:22.165 --> 00:29:24.010
P was initialization.

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:26.740
So there's two things that
will happen when you derive

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:29.140
and you implement
mu P. One of them

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:30.890
will be the initialization
will change,

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:33.490
and the other thing will be
that the learning rate changes--

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:35.870
or the learning rate
changes per layer.

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:39.250
And that is probably a more
exotic object than many of you

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:40.690
are used to.

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:43.000
The initialization actually
is not that different.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:47.590
If you're already using a
standard like, Kaiming style

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:50.387
initialization, that's
already 1 over the fanin--

00:29:50.387 --> 00:29:51.970
1 over-- sorry, 1
over the square root

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:56.560
of the fanin, which is going
to be already the right thing.

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:58.525
Whereas the learning
rate normally

00:29:58.525 --> 00:30:00.650
like-- unless you're doing
something really exotic,

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:03.560
you're using a global constant
learning rate everywhere.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.190
So that's going to be a
big difference from what

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:08.437
you're normally training with.

00:30:08.437 --> 00:30:11.020
So you can think of that as the
practical difference for a lot

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:13.390
of the mu P implementations.

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:14.405
Yes.

00:30:14.405 --> 00:30:16.155
[INAUDIBLE] I think
that was kept constant

00:30:16.155 --> 00:30:19.400
[INAUDIBLE] He
saw that the curve

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:27.330
was very close to the
cosine decay [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:28.790
So you're talking
about this curve.

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:30.560
And you're saying
like oh, when we're

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:33.360
in the stable phase of
WSD, when we're up here,

00:30:33.360 --> 00:30:36.240
the curve remains pretty
close, and why is that.

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:38.700
Well, it's close,
but also not really.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:42.628
If you look at this
last curve over here,

00:30:42.628 --> 00:30:44.420
there's a big gap before
we enter the decay

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:47.540
phase between cosine and WSD.

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:50.810
And I think this is one of the
pretty interesting mysteries

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:53.270
about deep learning optimizers.

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:56.660
Clearly you need a
stable phase to get you

00:30:56.660 --> 00:30:58.290
far from your initialization.

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:00.680
But also, the cool
down phase is what

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:02.810
gets you most of your
gains and your losses.

00:31:02.810 --> 00:31:06.740
If you don't cool down, this is
a gigantic loss in your losses.

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:09.150
So the cool down is
actually really critical.

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:10.760
And a lot of the
gains from cosine

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:14.270
versus here, this relative gap,
this is all from cool down.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:18.070
And so a lot of the optimizer
learning rate design

00:31:18.070 --> 00:31:20.140
is about this
balance between how

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:22.120
do I keep learning
rates high to travel far

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:24.130
from my initialization,
but still

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:27.100
have good decay on
my learning rate

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:32.200
to be able to anneal my loss
down to a very low value.

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:36.850
So the other paper I want
to talk about is DeepSeek.

00:31:36.850 --> 00:31:42.370
This is the original
DeepSeek LLM paper from 2024.

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:47.000
And in many ways, if you read
the original DeepSeek LLM paper,

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:51.490
you can know that these are
very serious science people

00:31:51.490 --> 00:31:54.560
because they do a lot of very
careful scaling ablations,

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:57.590
and they're really trying to
get it right when they scale up.

00:31:57.590 --> 00:32:02.500
And that's an attitude that's
shared amongst the players that

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:04.360
get scaling right.

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:08.380
So they have 7 and
67B parameter models.

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:10.750
At the time, very
high performance

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:13.870
relative to Llama, which is
really the primary competitor

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:15.130
at the time.

00:32:15.130 --> 00:32:19.640
And at the time, I guess,
Llama 2 and Mistral

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:21.560
were the big players,
DeepSeek comes in

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:23.310
and they're able to
match the performance.

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:25.400
Not quite the flashy
impact of DeepSeek

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:30.590
v3 coming in and matching
OpenAI's GPT 4.0.

00:32:30.590 --> 00:32:33.290
But for a first
time attempt, this

00:32:33.290 --> 00:32:34.710
is a pretty remarkable result.

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:37.280
And so let's dig in
and try to understand,

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:38.990
what did DeepSeek
do that allowed

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:43.160
them to go from
essentially 0 to at least

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:48.065
for open source state
of the art at the time.

00:32:48.065 --> 00:32:53.120
And I think DeepSeek more
than most other players, maybe

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:55.100
the only comparable
one being MiniCPM

00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:59.240
is very, very open about a lot
of the experiments they did

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:01.490
and the approach
they used to choose

00:33:01.490 --> 00:33:03.770
a lot of these hyperparameters.

00:33:03.770 --> 00:33:07.820
So immediately we see one
difference between DeepSeek v1

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:11.570
and MiniCPM, and
also Cerebras-GPT,

00:33:11.570 --> 00:33:13.695
which is that they
don't use any mu P.

00:33:13.695 --> 00:33:15.070
And they're going
to directly try

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:16.690
to estimate both
the optimal batch

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:19.430
size and the optimal
learning rate.

00:33:19.430 --> 00:33:22.240
So it's like a really direct
method, you might call it,

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:26.110
and requires a strong
belief in scaling laws.

00:33:26.110 --> 00:33:33.010
So what they do is they take
two relatively small models,

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:36.203
and they run a grid over
different batch sizes,

00:33:36.203 --> 00:33:38.120
they run a grid over
different learning rates,

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:41.540
and they get losses
across this grid.

00:33:41.540 --> 00:33:44.000
They do the same thing
at a larger scale.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:48.910
And you can get the optimum
batch size and learning rate.

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:51.400
And so they're saying
all right, well, this

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:53.980
is a pretty wide basin,
so we don't maybe

00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:58.090
have to be too scared
about messing this up.

00:33:58.090 --> 00:34:00.220
And so then what
they do is they know

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:02.770
that the choice of learning
rate and batch size

00:34:02.770 --> 00:34:04.990
are both relatively
forgiving, but we

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:07.300
do want to get the order of
magnitude of these things

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:07.910
correct.

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:09.940
So how do we get the order
of magnitude of these things

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:10.550
correct?

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:12.250
Well, what we're
going to do is we're

00:34:12.250 --> 00:34:15.600
going to train a bunch of
models with different amounts

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:21.060
of nonembedding flops, and we're
going to change essentially

00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:24.730
across a grid the parameters
that I had before,

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:26.590
both the batch size
and the learning rate.

00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:28.710
And by varying
these, we're going

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:32.280
to have the optimum batch size
and the optimum learning rate,

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:34.179
sorry, across these
different scales.

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:36.239
So you can imagine
basically making these grids

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:38.610
across many different
flops scales,

00:34:38.610 --> 00:34:43.469
and basically marking
down a star for each one.

00:34:43.469 --> 00:34:46.659
Perhaps unsurprisingly, because
it's a scaling law lectures,

00:34:46.659 --> 00:34:51.179
these things seem to
follow a scaling law line.

00:34:51.179 --> 00:34:54.699
At least for the batch size
things seem more clear.

00:34:54.699 --> 00:34:57.882
And you can fit a line to here,
and you can extrapolate out

00:34:57.882 --> 00:34:59.340
to the big models
that you're going

00:34:59.340 --> 00:35:03.990
to train what your optimal
batch sizes should look like.

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:06.183
They do the same thing
with learning rate.

00:35:06.183 --> 00:35:08.100
And they fit this line
and they say, oh, these

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:10.380
are the two learning
rates we're going to use.

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.190
It might be because
the points are being

00:35:12.190 --> 00:35:13.430
plotted on top of each other.

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:16.270
But I find this line to be
particularly-- not particularly,

00:35:16.270 --> 00:35:17.540
somewhat suspicious looking.

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:19.570
I mean, I could probably
fit a horizontal line,

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:21.320
and that would have
also looked OK.

00:35:21.320 --> 00:35:22.550
This one, I don't know.

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:27.490
Even as a scaling law
enthusiast, I'm not quite sure.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:30.530
I would bet my life on this
one to pick the learning rate.

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:34.540
But they did, and that's how
they get the learning rate.

00:35:34.540 --> 00:35:38.980
Now, they also follow best
practices at the time.

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:40.960
They do a Chinchilla
style analysis.

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:44.320
And they use, once again,
a WSD style learning rate,

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.690
where they are trying
to essentially minimize

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:49.690
the amount of repeated
work that they do.

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:53.560
They do something a little
bit weird or a little bit more

00:35:53.560 --> 00:35:56.930
nonstandard where what they're
doing is they do warmup,

00:35:56.930 --> 00:36:00.280
they do stable, and then they do
two sets of decay steps decaying

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:01.100
down to 0.

00:36:01.100 --> 00:36:06.800
So it's like two decay phases
consisting of 10% plus 10%.

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:10.430
And they analyze different
choices of that decay phase.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:12.990
And they doesn't seem
to matter very much.

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:15.470
But generally speaking,
it's about 20%

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:17.180
of the total compute
budget is going

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:20.210
to be spent on that
cool down phase.

00:36:20.210 --> 00:36:22.960
And so they also show once again
that it matches cosine learning

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:23.460
rates.

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:24.980
But once again,
the advantage here

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:30.530
is that we can do Chinchilla
style analysis for very cheap.

00:36:30.530 --> 00:36:32.670
In contrast to the
learning rate--

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:36.800
sorry, learning rate fits,
Chinchilla style analysis just

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:38.400
fits really, really cleanly.

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:40.130
I think this is a broad lesson.

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:42.720
When you look at lots
of people scaling laws,

00:36:42.720 --> 00:36:44.660
I think the stuff
on hyperparameters

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:48.680
always looks a little
noisy and tenuous.

00:36:48.680 --> 00:36:51.980
But the isoflops analysis
from all of the players

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:54.360
look always very, very nice.

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:57.810
And so this is replication
of the Chinchilla result.

00:36:57.810 --> 00:37:00.300
You see different
compute scales.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:01.740
We see different quadratics.

00:37:01.740 --> 00:37:03.990
We draw a line through the
bottom of the quadratics.

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:08.955
We get exactly the optimum of--

00:37:08.955 --> 00:37:12.310
I'm sorry, optimum flops
per token and optimum token

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:14.810
size as a function
of training flops.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:16.750
So this gives us a very
straightforward way

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:21.040
of analyzing the token size
to model size trade-offs.

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:23.510
And this allows them to do
everything from scratch,

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:24.730
of course, I think.

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:28.180
As a side commentary, I think
it's really nice that they're

00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:29.540
redoing a lot of this.

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:32.740
They could have certainly
cargo culted Chinchilla

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:34.640
and just pick 20
tokens per parameter.

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:37.720
But they said no, let's actually
go and do the scaling law

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:39.190
analysis, and
let's actually make

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:44.685
sure that the token sizes are
relatively appropriate for us.

00:37:47.275 --> 00:37:50.740
And then they have a fitted
scaling law at the very end.

00:37:50.740 --> 00:37:52.377
This is-- in some
ways unsurprising

00:37:52.377 --> 00:37:54.710
because this is after they
fixed their scaling strategy.

00:37:54.710 --> 00:37:56.120
They do predictable scaling.

00:37:56.120 --> 00:38:00.490
They try to predict what happens
on the 7B and the 67B models.

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:03.430
It's unsurprising in
many ways, but very nice

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:06.950
that they're able to extrapolate
out from about 10 to the 20--

00:38:06.950 --> 00:38:10.280
the 10 to the 24, and
actually nail the prediction

00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:12.050
on the basis of the scaling law.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:15.500
So it's a very nice thing to be
able to see that we can actually

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:21.060
get predictive measures
of model capabilities

00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:22.350
before we actually train them.

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:25.170
So that's the DeepSeek part.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:27.950
Anyone have questions
for the DeepSeek strategy

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:30.750
and what they did and
any of the other pieces?

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:32.340
I think most of this, I think--

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:35.923
WSD was probably the newest
thing that I've mentioned today.

00:38:35.923 --> 00:38:37.340
The other thing
that DeepSeek does

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:39.530
is directly fitting a
scaling law on to the optimum

00:38:39.530 --> 00:38:42.140
learning rate and batch sizes
rather than using something

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:45.440
like mu P. Yes.

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:48.570
Do they have a global learning
rate for all the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:50.070
Do they have a
global learning rate?

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:50.360
Yes.

00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:50.860
Yeah.

00:38:50.860 --> 00:38:52.420
So the tuning that
global learning.

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:56.420
Cool.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:57.230
OK.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:57.800
Yeah.

00:38:57.800 --> 00:39:05.360
Once we know the [INAUDIBLE] for
any frontier model [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:05.360 --> 00:39:05.960
Yeah.

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:07.540
So the question
was like, do people

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:10.960
redo this analysis for
new frontier models?

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:13.040
To be honest, I'm
not actually sure.

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:15.370
And I am beginning to
think that a lot of people

00:39:15.370 --> 00:39:19.780
maybe don't exactly replicate
some of this, because we see

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:22.320
that in the newer papers,
just increasingly less scaling

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:22.820
details.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:27.260
Even from DeepSeek, for example,
like DeepSeek v2 and v3,

00:39:27.260 --> 00:39:30.190
we see a lot of emphasis on
the new parts of each paper.

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:33.070
So for DeepSeek v2, we
see a lot of emphasis

00:39:33.070 --> 00:39:35.980
on MLA and the
architectural improvements.

00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:39.070
In the DeepSeek v3, we see a lot
of the systems components being

00:39:39.070 --> 00:39:41.620
emphasized, like the
low bit training.

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:43.510
But we don't see, for
example, in either

00:39:43.510 --> 00:39:46.730
of those, any additional
new scaling law studies.

00:39:46.730 --> 00:39:50.097
And so I think my guess is that
there's not much new there.

00:39:50.097 --> 00:39:52.430
Maybe they're replicating it
just to make sure it works,

00:39:52.430 --> 00:39:54.850
but nothing new to report.

00:39:54.850 --> 00:39:58.780
And I think that will be
captured in the next couple

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:01.900
slides where I'm going to talk
about scaling laws and papers

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:04.060
and models from the
last year or so.

00:40:04.060 --> 00:40:06.900
So I did a little brief survey.

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:08.820
But actually,
there's nothing that

00:40:08.820 --> 00:40:11.820
is at the level of detail of
either MiniCPM or DeepSeek

00:40:11.820 --> 00:40:14.430
Those are really still, I
think, the most detailed

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:19.440
open studies into scaling
that we have in 2025.

00:40:19.440 --> 00:40:20.310
Cool.

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:21.070
OK.

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:24.780
So Llama 3 was probably
one of the bigger model

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:28.350
releases in the past year
since I last taught this class.

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:31.090
And they do have some pretty
interesting scaling bits.

00:40:31.090 --> 00:40:34.260
For one, just the question
right now of like,

00:40:34.260 --> 00:40:36.690
do people actually
replicate these analyzes

00:40:36.690 --> 00:40:37.870
once they've run them once.

00:40:37.870 --> 00:40:44.910
Well, yes, Llama 3 redoes
the isoflop style scaling--

00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:46.090
Chinchilla scaling laws.

00:40:46.090 --> 00:40:49.560
And they find roughly
that the optimum ratio,

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:53.202
if I got the calculation
right, is about 39 to 1.

00:40:53.202 --> 00:40:55.110
And I do think
this is interesting

00:40:55.110 --> 00:40:59.350
because Chinchilla got the
20 to 1 parameter ratio.

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:02.430
I think many of us have trained
models at the Chinchilla ratio

00:41:02.430 --> 00:41:04.750
in our research and so on.

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:07.955
It's quite clear that the
20 isn't really that stable.

00:41:07.955 --> 00:41:09.580
Other people that
have been fighting it

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:12.340
have been getting generally
slightly higher ratios

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:13.840
than before.

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:16.150
And that might point
to things like improved

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:19.090
algorithmic efficiency
in architectures

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:21.130
that learn better from data.

00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:24.550
It might mean something else,
like improved data quality.

00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:26.452
All of those are moving parts.

00:41:26.452 --> 00:41:28.660
So it's hard to know what's
leading to these slightly

00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:29.630
different ratios.

00:41:29.630 --> 00:41:31.150
But the results
seem fairly clear,

00:41:31.150 --> 00:41:33.608
that the fits are relatively
good and they do get a 40 to 1

00:41:33.608 --> 00:41:34.960
ratio.

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:38.860
The other thing which is close
to the data scaling stuff

00:41:38.860 --> 00:41:42.670
that I mentioned, the early
parts of my first scaling

00:41:42.670 --> 00:41:43.900
lecture.

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:46.630
One of the interesting things
that the Llama 3 folks do

00:41:46.630 --> 00:41:54.590
is they try to essentially
compute into NLLS like log loss,

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:59.270
and then correlate those NLLS
back into downstream accuracies.

00:41:59.270 --> 00:42:02.230
And so the thinking that
they're trying to do here

00:42:02.230 --> 00:42:05.610
is they would like to not
really scale against log

00:42:05.610 --> 00:42:08.340
likelihoods, that's not really
a thing they truly care about.

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:10.200
They care about
improving, I don't

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:14.400
know, benchmark numbers on
MMLU, or lambada, or whatever

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:17.592
other benchmarks that they've
decided to hill climb on.

00:42:17.592 --> 00:42:20.340
And so if that's the case,
then what they're going to need

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:22.140
is to have a
conversion factor going

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:24.750
from these NLS per
character, these

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:27.220
perplexities or equivalent
to perplexities,

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:29.830
and then map them
into accuracies.

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:32.290
And so they've done
some studies in Llama 3,

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:34.870
essentially trying to relate
these two fitting sigmoids,

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:39.552
showing that if you fit
essentially these small models

00:42:39.552 --> 00:42:41.760
and you fit some Llama 2
models and you fit a sigmoid

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:43.710
on the whole thing, you
can accurately predict

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:47.860
the performance of Llama 3 405B
on the basis of those fits.

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:49.140
It's interesting.

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:53.310
I think they say that they use
these ideas for data selection.

00:42:53.310 --> 00:42:56.050
But I think there's not
that much details there.

00:42:56.050 --> 00:43:00.240
And it's unclear whether this is
like a really core object when

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:01.990
Llama 3 was being
trained, or whether this

00:43:01.990 --> 00:43:04.690
was a side scaling
thing that was just

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:05.820
of interest to the authors.

00:43:10.060 --> 00:43:12.640
Another recent work
that has come out,

00:43:12.640 --> 00:43:18.340
yet another Chinese LLM that's
nicely executed is Hunyuan-1.

00:43:18.340 --> 00:43:21.640
Hopefully I didn't really
butcher the pronunciation there.

00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:23.540
They are training MoEs.

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:25.580
And so because
they're training MoEs,

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:28.670
they want to redo the
Chinchilla style analysis.

00:43:28.670 --> 00:43:31.160
They fit-- once again,
they do isoform analysis.

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:32.090
They fit quadratics.

00:43:32.090 --> 00:43:34.120
They figure out the
minimums, and then they're

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:36.320
able to get a different
token to parameter ratio.

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:39.490
So they a 96 to 1 data to
active parameter ratio.

00:43:39.490 --> 00:43:41.740
These ratios are obviously
going to be quite different

00:43:41.740 --> 00:43:43.277
because the training
MoEs, there's

00:43:43.277 --> 00:43:45.110
lots of differences
about the architectures.

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:48.190
We don't really expect the
same thing as Chinchilla.

00:43:48.190 --> 00:43:50.927
And so we do actually
see in various papers,

00:43:50.927 --> 00:43:52.510
essentially replications
of Chinchilla

00:43:52.510 --> 00:43:54.970
happen again and again,
because a lot of these people

00:43:54.970 --> 00:43:56.710
are very interested
in understanding

00:43:56.710 --> 00:44:01.000
how far can I push the token
to parameter size ratio.

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:03.000
We would like to stay on
the higher end of that,

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:05.340
have more data than
parameters, because then people

00:44:05.340 --> 00:44:07.650
will actually use our
models or our models will

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:08.550
be cheap to serve.

00:44:08.550 --> 00:44:10.920
So for all those
reasons, people have

00:44:10.920 --> 00:44:12.580
been replicating Chinchilla.

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:16.260
I think this is one of the best
replicated results in scaling

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:17.310
in many ways.

00:44:17.310 --> 00:44:19.920
The actual 20 to
1 parameter ratio

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:22.150
isn't the thing that
consistently replicates.

00:44:22.150 --> 00:44:24.570
But the fact that you can do
isoflops and fit the minimum

00:44:24.570 --> 00:44:28.170
and get these very predictable
trade-offs in flops

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:36.900
to optimum parameters is
quite clean and consistent

00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:37.956
in the replications.

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:44.670
The last one, which is
honestly a little bit more

00:44:44.670 --> 00:44:49.620
of an exotic scaling
law over the last year

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:53.640
is MiniMax-01, which
came out pretty recently.

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:59.110
So MiniMax-01 is a linear
time or long context language

00:44:59.110 --> 00:45:03.820
model released by
another Chinese startup.

00:45:03.820 --> 00:45:05.762
And their interest
is, well, what

00:45:05.762 --> 00:45:08.470
we're going to do is we're going
to take softmax attention, which

00:45:08.470 --> 00:45:10.990
is quadratic, and
they have this thing

00:45:10.990 --> 00:45:14.530
called lightning attention,
which is a linear attention

00:45:14.530 --> 00:45:18.550
or linear yeah, linear attention
layer, which is linear time.

00:45:18.550 --> 00:45:21.110
And then they have a hybrid
version of this model.

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:22.870
And they want to figure
out how much cost

00:45:22.870 --> 00:45:26.170
am I paying in terms of the
performance of the model going

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:29.840
from softmax to linear
to hybrid attention.

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.530
And so they do things like--
they basically replicate method

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:34.360
one for Chinchilla,
where they're

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:36.980
looking at the lower
envelope of the loss curves.

00:45:36.980 --> 00:45:38.710
As they train, they
look at essentially

00:45:38.710 --> 00:45:41.950
the implied optimal model size
and the implied optimal token

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:43.275
count as they go.

00:45:43.275 --> 00:45:45.400
And roughly the conclusion
that they draw from this

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:49.840
is that the lightning and
the hybrid models, roughly

00:45:49.840 --> 00:45:52.330
perform the same as
the softmax attention.

00:45:52.330 --> 00:45:57.120
And thus, they're OK to train
long context models on the basis

00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:59.220
of these architectures.

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:03.520
We've seen these plots occur
very often in research papers.

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:07.050
If you look at the Mamba
paper or the Mamba-2 paper,

00:46:07.050 --> 00:46:10.890
or the delta net paper or any
of these other linear time

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:12.738
complexity RNN
papers, you'll see

00:46:12.738 --> 00:46:14.280
plots that look a
lot like this where

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:18.600
they say, oh, the full attention
scaling and my linear attention

00:46:18.600 --> 00:46:21.340
scaling are basically the
same as a function of compute.

00:46:21.340 --> 00:46:25.590
But this is-- I would say, a
rare case of this same plot

00:46:25.590 --> 00:46:32.000
being produced almost at scale
from a major artifact release.

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:36.220
OK.

00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:38.490
So putting all that
together, I know

00:46:38.490 --> 00:46:40.920
that was a bunch of
mini case studies

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:43.800
that I went through
fairly quickly.

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:47.220
But I want to step back
and recap it a little bit.

00:46:47.220 --> 00:46:49.350
We've seen several
common ingredients being

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:51.240
used in these scaling recipes.

00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:54.120
We've seen Cerebras,
DeepSeek, MiniCPM, and then

00:46:54.120 --> 00:46:56.530
the few new paper since.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:47:00.130
So Cerebras and
MiniCPM both use mu P

00:47:00.130 --> 00:47:01.900
as a way to make
hyperparameters more

00:47:01.900 --> 00:47:06.340
stable across scale, and they--

00:47:06.340 --> 00:47:11.600
MiniCPM, especially,
has a nice WSD schedule,

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:14.320
which is a thing they popularize
to be able to do Chinchilla

00:47:14.320 --> 00:47:15.190
style scaling.

00:47:15.190 --> 00:47:18.130
Cerebras doesn't bother
to replicate Chinchilla.

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:20.270
DeepSeek does a little
bit different thing.

00:47:20.270 --> 00:47:22.390
They assume that most
hyperparameters just

00:47:22.390 --> 00:47:23.540
don't change with scale.

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:25.900
But they do a full scaling
analysis on batch size

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:28.660
and learning rate, and then they
use the scaling laws as a way

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:30.370
to figure out optimal scaling.

00:47:30.370 --> 00:47:32.292
I've already noted that
some of the scaling

00:47:32.292 --> 00:47:34.250
looks a little bit more
suspicious than others.

00:47:34.250 --> 00:47:36.917
But really, this is a way to at
least get the order of magnitude

00:47:36.917 --> 00:47:37.930
hopefully right.

00:47:37.930 --> 00:47:39.690
They use isoflops analysis.

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:42.190
They replicate Chinchilla once
again to figure out the model

00:47:42.190 --> 00:47:44.920
sizing, and to make sure they're
in the right order of magnitude.

00:47:44.920 --> 00:47:48.040
Of the more recent releases,
Llama 3 and Hunyuan

00:47:48.040 --> 00:47:51.760
do isoflops analysis only.

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:54.290
Llama 3 does a little bit
more, but that's basically it.

00:47:54.290 --> 00:47:56.440
And then MiniMax does the
more interesting thing

00:47:56.440 --> 00:47:59.470
of basically justifying
architecture choices

00:47:59.470 --> 00:48:01.070
through the lens
of a scaling law.

00:48:01.070 --> 00:48:04.000
But we see, generally
speaking, that there's

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:06.820
a few different things that
get replicated like Chinchilla,

00:48:06.820 --> 00:48:08.775
and learning rate and
batch size are really

00:48:08.775 --> 00:48:10.150
the things that
people are really

00:48:10.150 --> 00:48:12.590
deeply concerned about when
they're scaling models up.

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:14.560
And they do things
like fixed aspect ratio

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:16.253
and just scale the
total model size up.

00:48:16.253 --> 00:48:17.920
And that's generally
the way that people

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:23.950
handle a lot of the moving
pieces of scaling up.

00:48:23.950 --> 00:48:26.153
Any questions about the
case studies pieces?

00:48:26.153 --> 00:48:27.820
Actually I'm going
to stay here and just

00:48:27.820 --> 00:48:30.970
make sure I've covered
any questions that people

00:48:30.970 --> 00:48:31.515
might have.

00:48:38.230 --> 00:48:38.930
OK.

00:48:38.930 --> 00:48:39.430
Cool.

00:48:42.370 --> 00:48:45.850
So the second and last
part of this lecture

00:48:45.850 --> 00:48:48.550
is going to be understanding
mu P. Hopefully

00:48:48.550 --> 00:48:51.640
through the case studies you've
seen that essentially getting

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:54.170
the learning rate right is
one of the core concerns

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:57.180
that people have and
also the batch size.

00:48:57.180 --> 00:48:59.990
But in general, I think we
want to have scale invariant

00:48:59.990 --> 00:49:01.110
hyperparameters.

00:49:01.110 --> 00:49:03.890
And it is the case that our
choice of initialization

00:49:03.890 --> 00:49:05.870
and our choice of per
layer learning rates

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:07.310
are essentially arbitrary.

00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:09.770
There's no reason why
we have to initialize

00:49:09.770 --> 00:49:11.720
them one way and not the other.

00:49:11.720 --> 00:49:15.650
And so if we could manipulate
those free variables to get

00:49:15.650 --> 00:49:18.050
scale and variance in
our learning rates,

00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:19.550
that would just be
really wonderful.

00:49:19.550 --> 00:49:21.680
That would make our
lives way easier.

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:25.340
And it would make small scale
experiments much more possible.

00:49:25.340 --> 00:49:28.550
So I'll also talk
first through the math

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:30.270
of this, how it's derived?

00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:31.560
What's the justification?

00:49:31.560 --> 00:49:33.110
What are the core
conceptual objects

00:49:33.110 --> 00:49:35.280
behind trying to make
models scale predictably?

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:39.290
And then I want to talk
about a pretty nice preprint

00:49:39.290 --> 00:49:42.050
by an independent
researcher on basically just

00:49:42.050 --> 00:49:45.630
a bunch of ablations on
mu P. What makes it break?

00:49:45.630 --> 00:49:47.600
What does a robust do?

00:49:47.600 --> 00:49:50.730
Does it work on a real
transformer language model?

00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:54.180
These questions are explored
pretty well in this preprint

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:57.450
that I'll talk about
at the very end here.

00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:58.450
So OK.

00:49:58.450 --> 00:50:00.040
What is mu P anyway?

00:50:00.040 --> 00:50:02.550
I feel like maybe I've jumped
the gun for the last two

00:50:02.550 --> 00:50:05.850
lectures because I've mentioned
what this is without really

00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:09.690
giving you the core conceptual
object that it's based off of.

00:50:09.690 --> 00:50:12.180
On the other hand, I think
I'm justified in doing this

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:13.763
because I think most
of the literature

00:50:13.763 --> 00:50:16.210
doesn't explain mu P
that clearly either.

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:18.690
They're just like, yeah, just
scale the initialization by 1

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:21.065
over the width and scale the
per layer learning rate by 1

00:50:21.065 --> 00:50:22.890
over the width, that's mu P.

00:50:22.890 --> 00:50:25.350
But I think the
ideas behind mu P

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:27.190
are pretty interesting
and worth discussing,

00:50:27.190 --> 00:50:30.510
because I think they speak to
some core objects that recur

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:32.940
in deep learning in general.

00:50:32.940 --> 00:50:37.440
So I'm going to be basing my
slides off of this preprint

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:38.230
or paper.

00:50:38.230 --> 00:50:40.770
If you're interested
in reading about mu P,

00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:42.070
I would point you to this one.

00:50:42.070 --> 00:50:45.870
I think this and
another blog post

00:50:45.870 --> 00:50:47.820
called a practitioner
guides to mu P,

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:53.000
I think are the two
readable descriptions

00:50:53.000 --> 00:50:55.306
of what this paradigm is.

00:50:55.306 --> 00:50:57.830
So I'm going to base
myself off this.

00:50:57.830 --> 00:50:59.353
The math is, for
whatever reason,

00:50:59.353 --> 00:51:01.770
not exactly the same across
these different presentations,

00:51:01.770 --> 00:51:04.790
so I'll clarify that I'm basing
the math off of this one.

00:51:04.790 --> 00:51:08.720
So mu P is based off of the
following relatively simple

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:09.482
ideas.

00:51:09.482 --> 00:51:10.940
So there's two
things that we think

00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:14.362
should happen when we're
training a neural network.

00:51:14.362 --> 00:51:17.927
So when we scalarize a neural
network, we're going to make,

00:51:17.927 --> 00:51:19.760
in this case, let's
just say only the width,

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.180
the width of the network bigger.

00:51:21.180 --> 00:51:23.822
And we're going to fix the
layer size or sorry, the depth,

00:51:23.822 --> 00:51:26.030
and then I'm going to make
the width bigger as we go.

00:51:26.030 --> 00:51:29.340
Now if I do that, as I
make the width bigger,

00:51:29.340 --> 00:51:32.270
I want the activations
at initialization

00:51:32.270 --> 00:51:35.400
to remain big theta of 1.

00:51:35.400 --> 00:51:38.930
I want it to remain
roughly constant bounded

00:51:38.930 --> 00:51:40.760
above and below by
universal constant,

00:51:40.760 --> 00:51:42.955
roughly constant as I
make the width bigger.

00:51:42.955 --> 00:51:43.830
It shouldn't blow up.

00:51:43.830 --> 00:51:45.672
It shouldn't vanish.

00:51:45.672 --> 00:51:47.480
Seems like a pretty
natural thing to want.

00:51:47.480 --> 00:51:49.438
You don't want your
activations to get too big.

00:51:49.438 --> 00:51:51.240
This is per coordinate.

00:51:51.240 --> 00:51:53.160
Now, the second
assertion I want is

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:54.850
that I'm going to
initialize my model,

00:51:54.850 --> 00:51:56.530
and I'm going to take
a single gradient step.

00:51:56.530 --> 00:51:58.330
And when I take that
single gradient step,

00:51:58.330 --> 00:52:00.390
I want to make sure that
the change in activation

00:52:00.390 --> 00:52:04.114
should also be big theta of 1.

00:52:04.114 --> 00:52:07.450
So both of these seem
very natural conditions

00:52:07.450 --> 00:52:09.060
because if you
violate these, it's

00:52:09.060 --> 00:52:11.620
going to mean that as I
make the models bigger,

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:14.650
either the initial activations
will blow up or vanish,

00:52:14.650 --> 00:52:17.040
or after one gradient
step, my activations will

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:18.220
either blow up or vanish.

00:52:18.220 --> 00:52:22.170
Those are both bad,
bad conditions.

00:52:22.170 --> 00:52:24.810
And as a note, I'm talking
about individual activations

00:52:24.810 --> 00:52:25.780
like coordinates.

00:52:25.780 --> 00:52:27.630
And so if you're
thinking about norms

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:30.015
of an entire vector
of activations,

00:52:30.015 --> 00:52:33.852
that should look big theta
of square root of nl.

00:52:33.852 --> 00:52:35.310
Because each one
of these are going

00:52:35.310 --> 00:52:37.470
to be roughly independent,
so the norm is going

00:52:37.470 --> 00:52:40.240
to look like the square
root of the width,

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:45.570
the number of elements
in my width coordinate.

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:51.120
So I can derive mu P from
those two conditions.

00:52:51.120 --> 00:52:54.950
So the first condition, which
is that I want my activation

00:52:54.950 --> 00:52:58.280
to remain stable,
imposes constraints

00:52:58.280 --> 00:53:00.510
on the initialization.

00:53:00.510 --> 00:53:03.690
So I'm going to walk you through
a very, very simple example.

00:53:03.690 --> 00:53:06.750
So I'm going to consider
a deep linear network.

00:53:06.750 --> 00:53:09.470
So this is h of l, so
this is the activations

00:53:09.470 --> 00:53:10.992
at layer little l.

00:53:10.992 --> 00:53:12.950
And that's going to be
a function of the weight

00:53:12.950 --> 00:53:16.100
matrix at layer l,
and the activations

00:53:16.100 --> 00:53:17.370
from the previous layer.

00:53:17.370 --> 00:53:20.870
No nonlinearities,
no fancy stuff.

00:53:20.870 --> 00:53:22.140
It's all square.

00:53:22.140 --> 00:53:23.640
Just forget all
this complexities.

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:25.932
If you want complexities,
you can go read the preprint,

00:53:25.932 --> 00:53:28.070
they will explain in
slightly hand-wavy terms

00:53:28.070 --> 00:53:30.050
why those things don't matter.

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:32.840
Now, the initialization,
I'm going to pick a Gaussian

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:33.780
initialization.

00:53:33.780 --> 00:53:35.310
So it's going to
be zero-centered.

00:53:35.310 --> 00:53:39.980
It's going to be a rectangular
size of the sizes that depend

00:53:39.980 --> 00:53:44.060
on the sizes of my activations.

00:53:44.060 --> 00:53:46.620
And then I'm going to have
one hyperparameter, which

00:53:46.620 --> 00:53:51.130
is the noise scale of
this matrix at this layer.

00:53:51.130 --> 00:53:54.510
Sorry, there should be a
little l on this sigma.

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:56.260
So now what can we say?

00:53:56.260 --> 00:53:59.610
Well, I want to understand
the size of h of l

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:01.060
at initialization.

00:54:01.060 --> 00:54:02.580
So how can we do that?

00:54:02.580 --> 00:54:04.200
Well, one thing we
can do is we can

00:54:04.200 --> 00:54:06.780
consider the limiting
behavior of this system.

00:54:06.780 --> 00:54:09.900
I'm going to take
basically little n of l

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:13.030
and little n of l
minus 1 to infinity.

00:54:13.030 --> 00:54:16.150
And if I do that, this w
is going to concentrate.

00:54:16.150 --> 00:54:18.150
It's a random Gaussian
matrix, if you remember

00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:20.080
your random matrix theory.

00:54:20.080 --> 00:54:22.995
Actually that's not a
prerequisite for the course.

00:54:22.995 --> 00:54:25.050
If you know some basic
random matrix theory,

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:28.500
you know that the operator
norm of a Gaussian matrix

00:54:28.500 --> 00:54:30.740
is going to roughly
concentrate to this object.

00:54:30.740 --> 00:54:32.490
It's going to be sigma,
which is the noise

00:54:32.490 --> 00:54:36.750
scale times the square root of
both of the coordinates added.

00:54:36.750 --> 00:54:39.540
And importantly, you
can write down roughly

00:54:39.540 --> 00:54:40.990
that this equivalence is true.

00:54:40.990 --> 00:54:43.950
So the activations at
layer l, the norm of that

00:54:43.950 --> 00:54:47.090
is going to be approximately
equal to the operator

00:54:47.090 --> 00:54:51.620
norm of Wl times the activation
norm of h of l minus 1.

00:54:51.620 --> 00:54:54.110
And this is roughly
assuming that W of l

00:54:54.110 --> 00:54:56.190
is independent of
h of l minus 1,

00:54:56.190 --> 00:54:57.750
which is true at initialization.

00:54:57.750 --> 00:55:00.050
So I think you can basically
make that a right arrow

00:55:00.050 --> 00:55:01.610
if you'd like.

00:55:01.610 --> 00:55:02.990
Now, I'm going to say--

00:55:02.990 --> 00:55:05.660
I'm going to pick a particular
choice of sigma, which

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:08.360
is going to be square root
nl over square root nl

00:55:08.360 --> 00:55:11.130
minus 1 times this object.

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:14.060
You can simply think of it as
this right-hand side thing.

00:55:14.060 --> 00:55:15.810
This is the exact form.

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:18.720
This is the more asymptotic form
that you can think of this as.

00:55:18.720 --> 00:55:20.990
But really it's just
1 over the square root

00:55:20.990 --> 00:55:25.350
of the fanin in of your layer
times the minimum of one,

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:28.890
and the aspect
ratio of your model.

00:55:28.890 --> 00:55:32.945
In case your fanin n is much
larger than your fanout,

00:55:32.945 --> 00:55:35.190
then it kicks in.

00:55:35.190 --> 00:55:37.850
So let's say that I pick
this sigma, what happens?

00:55:37.850 --> 00:55:40.200
Roughly 1 over the
square root of my fanin.

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:41.040
So now what happens?

00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:44.470
I can plug this back
in to this formula,

00:55:44.470 --> 00:55:47.610
the matrix concentration limit
and also this approximation

00:55:47.610 --> 00:55:48.190
here.

00:55:48.190 --> 00:55:50.940
And I can inductively
prove that every layer

00:55:50.940 --> 00:55:53.680
is going to have the
right activation size.

00:55:53.680 --> 00:55:55.380
So let's just go
through all the layers.

00:55:55.380 --> 00:55:58.840
And assume that up until layer
l minus 1 I have this property

00:55:58.840 --> 00:56:01.620
So that's the inductive
assumption at layer l minus 1,

00:56:01.620 --> 00:56:06.080
I have that my activation norm
is square root of nl minus 1.

00:56:06.080 --> 00:56:06.580
OK.

00:56:06.580 --> 00:56:07.788
So that's just an assumption.

00:56:07.788 --> 00:56:11.470
Now, if this is true, then
I just plug all of these in.

00:56:11.470 --> 00:56:14.580
So I plug-in square root of
nl minus 1 into this component

00:56:14.580 --> 00:56:17.410
into Wl operator norm,
I plug-in the limit.

00:56:17.410 --> 00:56:20.260
And then for sigma, I plug-in
this expression over here.

00:56:20.260 --> 00:56:22.060
You see that this
inverse cancels this.

00:56:22.060 --> 00:56:26.850
And then you're going to
get exactly that h of l--

00:56:26.850 --> 00:56:31.300
the l2 norm of h of l is equal
to square root of n of l.

00:56:31.300 --> 00:56:34.260
So this is the thing that we
wanted because before, remember,

00:56:34.260 --> 00:56:38.080
we said, we want to make sure
that the activations remains

00:56:38.080 --> 00:56:40.080
big theta of 1, which
means that the norm should

00:56:40.080 --> 00:56:43.110
be square root of n of l.

00:56:43.110 --> 00:56:47.130
So that's exactly what we get
plus some lower order terms.

00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:51.720
So this is a fairly clear step
by step argument that shows you

00:56:51.720 --> 00:56:53.010
what the right thing to do is.

00:56:53.010 --> 00:56:55.105
For initializations,
I want to pick 1

00:56:55.105 --> 00:56:57.480
over the square root of the
fanin plus a small correction

00:56:57.480 --> 00:57:00.870
factor, in order to make sure
that my activations do not

00:57:00.870 --> 00:57:03.372
blow up at initialization.

00:57:03.372 --> 00:57:06.310
I'll pause here for a moment
in case someone has questions.

00:57:06.310 --> 00:57:09.240
I feel like this is actually
maybe the first real math

00:57:09.240 --> 00:57:10.450
that we've done in the class.

00:57:10.450 --> 00:57:13.248
So maybe it's a bit of a
context switch for people.

00:57:13.248 --> 00:57:15.540
I did not warn you that I
was going to talk about a bit

00:57:15.540 --> 00:57:16.040
of math.

00:57:20.640 --> 00:57:23.200
Is this all relatively
clear for people?

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:24.820
1 over square root of fanin.

00:57:24.820 --> 00:57:25.800
Yes.

00:57:25.800 --> 00:57:26.470
OK.

00:57:26.470 --> 00:57:28.595
I'm going to assume that
everyone's on board with 1

00:57:28.595 --> 00:57:29.970
over square root of fanin.

00:57:29.970 --> 00:57:35.070
So now we're going to derive
the second part of mu P.

00:57:35.070 --> 00:57:38.500
So the first part of mu P
was about initializations.

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:43.500
The second part of mu P is going
to be about learning rates.

00:57:43.500 --> 00:57:46.020
And so how are we going to
think about learning rates?

00:57:46.020 --> 00:57:47.520
Well, to think about
learning rates,

00:57:47.520 --> 00:57:49.270
I'm going to look at
the second condition.

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:52.200
The second condition A2 which
says, when I take one gradient

00:57:52.200 --> 00:57:54.930
step past initialization,
what needs to happen

00:57:54.930 --> 00:57:57.450
is that my activation--
sorry, my update size

00:57:57.450 --> 00:57:58.570
needs to remain constant.

00:57:58.570 --> 00:57:59.500
It can't blow up.

00:57:59.500 --> 00:58:01.025
It can't vanish.

00:58:01.025 --> 00:58:02.670
So what does that mean?

00:58:02.670 --> 00:58:08.280
So if I have an update of delta
Wl on the weights at layer l,

00:58:08.280 --> 00:58:09.820
where does that come from?

00:58:09.820 --> 00:58:10.840
Well, that comes from--

00:58:10.840 --> 00:58:12.752
let's say I'm doing
SGD, that's going

00:58:12.752 --> 00:58:13.960
to come from this expression.

00:58:13.960 --> 00:58:18.010
It's going to be a learning
rate times l which is my loss--

00:58:18.010 --> 00:58:20.730
the gradient of l which is my
loss, and then the activations

00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:21.630
transposed.

00:58:21.630 --> 00:58:25.140
In the case that my batch size
is 1, this is a rank-one object.

00:58:25.140 --> 00:58:29.132
This is a rank-one
update to delta of l.

00:58:29.132 --> 00:58:32.620
And because it's rank-one,
there's a nice easy expression.

00:58:32.620 --> 00:58:37.290
The change of Wl times the
activation of the previous layer

00:58:37.290 --> 00:58:40.010
is equal to the
norm of the change

00:58:40.010 --> 00:58:42.590
in Wl, the operator
norm of this thing,

00:58:42.590 --> 00:58:46.010
times the l2 norm
of h of l minus 1.

00:58:46.010 --> 00:58:48.320
And now, combine
this with the fact

00:58:48.320 --> 00:58:51.807
that the change in activation
at layer l is this expression.

00:58:51.807 --> 00:58:53.640
You can convince yourself
that this is true.

00:58:53.640 --> 00:58:55.850
You can write this
out by figuring out

00:58:55.850 --> 00:58:59.090
what the actual final
activation is at layer l

00:58:59.090 --> 00:59:04.515
after the update in canceling
out wl h of l which is a shared

00:59:04.515 --> 00:59:05.640
term across left and right.

00:59:05.640 --> 00:59:07.490
Then you'll get this expression.

00:59:07.490 --> 00:59:10.650
You'll get that, what
is the update in h of l?

00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:12.350
This is the object
that we want to keep

00:59:12.350 --> 00:59:15.240
roughly square root of n of l.

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:16.470
The norm of this object.

00:59:16.470 --> 00:59:18.290
So let's look through
each of these terms

00:59:18.290 --> 00:59:20.120
and look at what the
magnitude of this is.

00:59:20.120 --> 00:59:26.017
The first term here wl delta h
of l minus 1, this we can assume

00:59:26.017 --> 00:59:28.350
is going to be controlled
from the inductive assumption,

00:59:28.350 --> 00:59:33.770
because this is exactly
the delta h of l

00:59:33.770 --> 00:59:36.320
that we have plus the
condition A1 argument.

00:59:36.320 --> 00:59:39.370
Condition A1 basically says--

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:43.660
sorry, condition A1 is going to
say that delta of h of l minus 1

00:59:43.660 --> 00:59:46.210
is going to be square
root nl, and then wl

00:59:46.210 --> 00:59:48.880
is going to maintain that norm.

00:59:48.880 --> 00:59:50.860
The more complicated
parts is going

00:59:50.860 --> 00:59:53.230
to be these two arguments,
the second and third terms

00:59:53.230 --> 00:59:55.780
that we have here,
delta Wl h of l minus 1

00:59:55.780 --> 00:59:58.730
and delta Wl delta hl minus 1.

00:59:58.730 --> 01:00:00.640
Sorry, that's
quite the mouthful.

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:02.930
They all have the same order
of magnitude, actually.

01:00:02.930 --> 01:00:05.097
And the only thing that we
need to really figure out

01:00:05.097 --> 01:00:06.170
is this expression here.

01:00:06.170 --> 01:00:08.770
What is the product of
the previous layer's norm

01:00:08.770 --> 01:00:11.957
times the operator
norm of delta Wl,

01:00:11.957 --> 01:00:14.290
because we don't really know
how big the update is going

01:00:14.290 --> 01:00:16.970
to be in the weight
matrix W. If we knew that,

01:00:16.970 --> 01:00:18.495
all very straightforward stuff.

01:00:21.430 --> 01:00:24.070
And so the remaining
argument is actually

01:00:24.070 --> 01:00:26.050
relatively straightforward.

01:00:26.050 --> 01:00:29.270
Even though this is actually a
complicated jumble of things,

01:00:29.270 --> 01:00:31.450
the intuition is
actually very clear.

01:00:31.450 --> 01:00:33.670
The intuition for
this says, OK, what

01:00:33.670 --> 01:00:35.030
do I really need to figure out?

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:36.740
The one thing I really
need to figure out

01:00:36.740 --> 01:00:38.730
is this expression here.

01:00:38.730 --> 01:00:41.520
How much does the weight
at layer l change?

01:00:41.520 --> 01:00:43.430
If I can figure
that out, then I can

01:00:43.430 --> 01:00:45.410
derive all of the
relevant quantities

01:00:45.410 --> 01:00:47.030
and solve for the learning rate.

01:00:47.030 --> 01:00:50.120
At a high level, that's
our strategy here.

01:00:50.120 --> 01:00:52.640
And so how can we
possibly figure out

01:00:52.640 --> 01:00:56.910
after one gradient step
how much delta Wl moves.

01:00:56.910 --> 01:00:58.970
That's really the key question.

01:00:58.970 --> 01:01:01.880
Well, there's an additional
sneaky assumption

01:01:01.880 --> 01:01:03.300
that then shows up here.

01:01:03.300 --> 01:01:05.160
And the assumption is
something like this.

01:01:05.160 --> 01:01:08.360
If our learning is well behaved,
then after a single gradient

01:01:08.360 --> 01:01:11.940
step, then the change
in the loss, delta of l,

01:01:11.940 --> 01:01:15.420
this quantity has to
also be big theta of 1.

01:01:15.420 --> 01:01:16.560
And why is that?

01:01:16.560 --> 01:01:19.950
Well, because we don't want
the size of our losses,

01:01:19.950 --> 01:01:21.740
the update, the
decrease in our losses

01:01:21.740 --> 01:01:25.550
to blow up or go to 0 as
the width goes to infinity.

01:01:25.550 --> 01:01:28.190
We want essentially our
improvement in losses

01:01:28.190 --> 01:01:30.630
to remain roughly the
same order of magnitude

01:01:30.630 --> 01:01:32.220
no matter how big
our models get.

01:01:32.220 --> 01:01:34.950
That's a stronger assumption
than what we've had before.

01:01:34.950 --> 01:01:37.490
But assuming that's true,
then essentially, we can say,

01:01:37.490 --> 01:01:40.930
OK, the change in the loss
is multiplying the gradient

01:01:40.930 --> 01:01:42.290
with the change in the weights.

01:01:42.290 --> 01:01:44.060
This left side is O of 1.

01:01:44.060 --> 01:01:49.010
We know how big this delta
of l should look like.

01:01:49.010 --> 01:01:52.640
So now-- or sorry, we know how
big this delta Wl looks like.

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:55.190
Now we can solve for
the gradient size.

01:01:55.190 --> 01:01:57.250
And once we have that,
we can plug that in here.

01:01:57.250 --> 01:01:58.810
We know delta Wl.

01:01:58.810 --> 01:02:00.980
We know the gradient of l.

01:02:00.980 --> 01:02:03.770
We know the size of h
of l from condition A1.

01:02:03.770 --> 01:02:05.840
And now we can solve
for the learning rate.

01:02:05.840 --> 01:02:08.200
And that's exactly what
you get at the bottom here.

01:02:08.200 --> 01:02:10.910
And if you work
through the arithmetic,

01:02:10.910 --> 01:02:12.400
the final result
that you get here

01:02:12.400 --> 01:02:14.710
is that the learning
rate for SGD

01:02:14.710 --> 01:02:17.180
is equal to the
fanout over the fanin.

01:02:17.180 --> 01:02:20.080
So lots of steps involved
and lots of substitution

01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:21.700
and slightly sketchy
big O notation

01:02:21.700 --> 01:02:24.210
being substituted into
the equations here.

01:02:24.210 --> 01:02:25.960
But once we do that,
we're going to end up

01:02:25.960 --> 01:02:28.570
with a very simple formula.

01:02:28.570 --> 01:02:30.400
Note that this is true for SGD.

01:02:30.400 --> 01:02:34.100
And those of you that have been
paying attention and staring

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:38.310
at this equation are probably
internally complaining.

01:02:38.310 --> 01:02:42.830
You're like, you have misled us
because in a transformer, well,

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:44.840
what's nl over nl minus 1?

01:02:44.840 --> 01:02:47.990
For an MLP, that
actually is like a 4

01:02:47.990 --> 01:02:51.660
because you've got a factor
of 4 between DFF and D model.

01:02:51.660 --> 01:02:53.550
And so this thing
doesn't really change.

01:02:53.550 --> 01:02:55.320
It's just a constant
in most models.

01:02:55.320 --> 01:02:58.490
Unless your aspect ratios are
like dramatically changing

01:02:58.490 --> 01:03:01.130
through your network.

01:03:01.130 --> 01:03:04.760
The reason why mu P is different
from standard parameterization

01:03:04.760 --> 01:03:06.630
is because this
derivation is for SGD,

01:03:06.630 --> 01:03:10.730
where the parameterizations
look very similar between mu P

01:03:10.730 --> 01:03:11.870
and SP.

01:03:11.870 --> 01:03:14.220
If you do the exact same
derivation for Adam,

01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:15.950
you're going to find
that actually you're

01:03:15.950 --> 01:03:17.825
going to get slightly
different things, which

01:03:17.825 --> 01:03:20.300
is that it's going to
be 1 over the fanin

01:03:20.300 --> 01:03:23.810
rather than the
fanout over the fanin.

01:03:23.810 --> 01:03:24.540
OK.

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:26.610
So here's the recap.

01:03:26.610 --> 01:03:28.940
I have dragged you
through, hopefully

01:03:28.940 --> 01:03:32.950
willingly, the derivation of
the basic what people call

01:03:32.950 --> 01:03:36.220
the spectral conditions
that define mu P.

01:03:36.220 --> 01:03:39.280
But now I will give you
the one slide high level

01:03:39.280 --> 01:03:40.580
takeaway of that result.

01:03:40.580 --> 01:03:44.120
So when we want to do
something like mu P,

01:03:44.120 --> 01:03:46.420
if we are following the
guidelines from before

01:03:46.420 --> 01:03:48.130
directly, what we
will end up with

01:03:48.130 --> 01:03:51.310
is the following blue
box initialization.

01:03:51.310 --> 01:03:53.680
You set yourself to 1 over
the square root of fanin

01:03:53.680 --> 01:03:55.780
times the correction
factor that's

01:03:55.780 --> 01:03:58.880
1, if your fanin is
smaller than your fanout,

01:03:58.880 --> 01:04:01.730
but square root of
the ratio, otherwise.

01:04:01.730 --> 01:04:04.030
And then this is a
simple initialization

01:04:04.030 --> 01:04:06.700
for the scale of your Gaussian.

01:04:06.700 --> 01:04:08.630
For your learning rate,
if you're doing SGD,

01:04:08.630 --> 01:04:11.423
then you set it to
fanout or fanin.

01:04:11.423 --> 01:04:14.090
But if you're doing Adam, that's
going to be slightly different.

01:04:14.090 --> 01:04:16.930
It's going to be
1 over the fanin.

01:04:16.930 --> 01:04:21.130
Now, in case you already know
the standard like Kaiming

01:04:21.130 --> 01:04:23.283
initialization and so on,
off the top of your head,

01:04:23.283 --> 01:04:24.700
you can mentally
compare what this

01:04:24.700 --> 01:04:26.863
looks like to the
standard parameterization.

01:04:26.863 --> 01:04:28.280
So in a standard
parameterization,

01:04:28.280 --> 01:04:30.400
if you're doing it right,
you should probably

01:04:30.400 --> 01:04:33.568
be already setting
your Gaussian's

01:04:33.568 --> 01:04:35.610
to initialize to 1 over
the square root of fanin.

01:04:35.610 --> 01:04:36.235
So that's good.

01:04:36.235 --> 01:04:38.060
That's already perfectly set.

01:04:38.060 --> 01:04:40.430
But your learning rates are
probably being set globally

01:04:40.430 --> 01:04:41.690
to a constant.

01:04:41.690 --> 01:04:44.970
This is fine for SGD,
not so fine for Adam

01:04:44.970 --> 01:04:52.100
where the really big difference
between SP and mu P comes in.

01:04:52.100 --> 01:04:53.300
So OK.

01:04:53.300 --> 01:04:56.808
That brings us right back
to the Cerebras-GPT paper.

01:04:56.808 --> 01:04:58.850
Now we have all the context
we need to understand

01:04:58.850 --> 01:05:00.770
all the operations they do.

01:05:00.770 --> 01:05:04.490
If you look, once again, at
the column over here of mu P,

01:05:04.490 --> 01:05:06.380
the embedding layer is special.

01:05:06.380 --> 01:05:09.110
It doesn't really do
essentially any scaling

01:05:09.110 --> 01:05:11.510
because embeddings are
one hot, so their norms

01:05:11.510 --> 01:05:15.360
don't scale linearly with
the number of vocab elements.

01:05:15.360 --> 01:05:17.570
But ignoring that,
basically you see

01:05:17.570 --> 01:05:21.770
that all the layers get scaled
down by 1 over the width, that's

01:05:21.770 --> 01:05:23.100
the initialization rule.

01:05:23.100 --> 01:05:26.990
And then the learning rate rules
are scaled by 1 over the width

01:05:26.990 --> 01:05:27.540
as well.

01:05:27.540 --> 01:05:30.947
So this is, once again, the
learning rate rule for Adam.

01:05:30.947 --> 01:05:33.530
So if you're using Adam, that's
exactly the right thing to do.

01:05:33.530 --> 01:05:37.760
And that's also exactly what
they do in Cerebras-GPT.

01:05:37.760 --> 01:05:39.430
So hopefully that's clear.

01:05:39.430 --> 01:05:40.930
And hopefully this
gives you a sense

01:05:40.930 --> 01:05:45.160
of both the interestingness of
manipulating per layer learning

01:05:45.160 --> 01:05:47.060
rates to get more
predictable scaling,

01:05:47.060 --> 01:05:49.570
and also maybe an
appreciation of this idea

01:05:49.570 --> 01:05:54.010
of trying to control
activations and updates

01:05:54.010 --> 01:05:55.850
as a function of model width.

01:05:55.850 --> 01:05:59.835
I'll pause for a
moment there and just

01:05:59.835 --> 01:06:02.050
that's like a very
successful idea from physics.

01:06:02.050 --> 01:06:04.850
Lots of physicists think about
ideas like renormalization.

01:06:04.850 --> 01:06:06.890
As I take limits
of certain things,

01:06:06.890 --> 01:06:08.330
I want things to remain stable.

01:06:08.330 --> 01:06:10.430
I want them to not
blow up or go to 0.

01:06:10.430 --> 01:06:12.410
This is an exact
application of that idea.

01:06:12.410 --> 01:06:15.680
That's an interesting
use of that.

01:06:15.680 --> 01:06:18.100
Any questions
about, I don't know,

01:06:18.100 --> 01:06:21.400
mu P derivation or Cerebras-GPT,
or any of the other things?

01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:22.540
Yes.

01:06:22.540 --> 01:06:24.610
[INAUDIBLE] question about
any architectural, so

01:06:24.610 --> 01:06:26.980
[INAUDIBLE] any other model.

01:06:26.980 --> 01:06:27.520
Yeah.

01:06:27.520 --> 01:06:29.700
So that is part of the subtlety.

01:06:29.700 --> 01:06:32.173
The question was, what's the
architecture assumptions.

01:06:32.173 --> 01:06:33.590
Well, I mean,
technically, there's

01:06:33.590 --> 01:06:35.460
an even stronger
assumption here--

01:06:35.460 --> 01:06:36.842
Oh, where did I go?

01:06:36.842 --> 01:06:38.550
There's an even stronger
assumption here,

01:06:38.550 --> 01:06:40.967
which is that I'm assuming
things are deep linear network.

01:06:40.967 --> 01:06:42.870
I'm just multiplying
matrices repeatedly.

01:06:42.870 --> 01:06:46.670
This is the silliest
network that you can have.

01:06:46.670 --> 01:06:49.520
Basically, there are arguments
for why adding nonlinearities

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:50.100
are fine.

01:06:50.100 --> 01:06:53.090
There are arguments for how you
would take the same arguments

01:06:53.090 --> 01:06:56.330
and apply them to
the attention layer.

01:06:56.330 --> 01:06:59.210
There are arguments for why
much more complex things are

01:06:59.210 --> 01:07:01.140
needed for a gated linear unit.

01:07:01.140 --> 01:07:03.260
So each one of those
architecture pieces

01:07:03.260 --> 01:07:05.000
needs a careful
analysis in order

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:08.960
to have a corresponding object.

01:07:08.960 --> 01:07:10.010
Yes.

01:07:10.010 --> 01:07:13.500
All these are like n
subnetworks determined,

01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:16.858
it looks like they are
indexed by data, right?

01:07:16.858 --> 01:07:17.400
You're right.

01:07:17.400 --> 01:07:21.470
So n sub l is just the
output of a matrix multiply,

01:07:21.470 --> 01:07:23.400
and nl minus 1 is the input.

01:07:23.400 --> 01:07:25.520
So for example, if
you have an Ml P

01:07:25.520 --> 01:07:27.950
you're going to have a matrix
multiply that takes you

01:07:27.950 --> 01:07:30.200
from D model dimension
to four times D model

01:07:30.200 --> 01:07:31.830
like the DFF dimension.

01:07:31.830 --> 01:07:35.640
So that would give you nl over
nl minus 1 of 4, for example.

01:07:35.640 --> 01:07:37.100
So all the different
matrix shapes

01:07:37.100 --> 01:07:39.800
are giving you the nl minus 1.

01:07:39.800 --> 01:07:40.680
oh, OK.

01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:41.900
It's like [INAUDIBLE].

01:07:41.900 --> 01:07:43.980
The fanin and the
fanout of a matrix.

01:07:43.980 --> 01:07:44.960
Yeah.

01:07:44.960 --> 01:07:46.880
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:46.880 --> 01:07:47.520
Exactly.

01:07:47.520 --> 01:07:47.870
Yeah.

01:07:47.870 --> 01:07:49.287
So the input and
output dimensions

01:07:49.287 --> 01:07:52.850
are determining all
of these objects.

01:07:52.850 --> 01:07:55.095
OK.

01:07:55.095 --> 01:07:55.595
Excellent.

01:07:55.595 --> 01:07:59.120
[INAUDIBLE] is a fanin
fanout the same idea.

01:07:59.120 --> 01:08:02.420
It's also just fanin
output of the [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:02.420 --> 01:08:05.740
Fanin and the fanout are like
the input and output dimensions.

01:08:05.740 --> 01:08:06.240
Yeah.

01:08:06.240 --> 01:08:07.782
I was using those
terms exchangeably,

01:08:07.782 --> 01:08:11.840
but I should have been
a little bit more clear.

01:08:11.840 --> 01:08:12.950
Oh, yes.

01:08:12.950 --> 01:08:16.310
Since DeepSeek uses a
global learning rate,

01:08:16.310 --> 01:08:19.520
does that mean that they
don't have order-one updates?

01:08:19.520 --> 01:08:22.220
So the question was like, since
DeepSeek uses a global learning

01:08:22.220 --> 01:08:24.649
rate, does that mean they
don't have an order-one update?

01:08:24.649 --> 01:08:27.859
So all of this
argument is asymptotic.

01:08:27.859 --> 01:08:31.290
It's basically saying, as I
scale my width out to infinity,

01:08:31.290 --> 01:08:33.750
things will be big or small.

01:08:33.750 --> 01:08:37.920
And I mean, if you look at
the mu P plot, for example,

01:08:37.920 --> 01:08:39.590
you do see this.

01:08:39.590 --> 01:08:41.779
You see that the
learning rates have

01:08:41.779 --> 01:08:43.997
to shift as the model
gets larger in order

01:08:43.997 --> 01:08:46.580
to compensate for the fact that
the updates are getting bigger

01:08:46.580 --> 01:08:48.140
and bigger.

01:08:48.140 --> 01:08:51.830
What's empirically been seen
is if you do nail the learning

01:08:51.830 --> 01:08:53.990
rate, you don't
need mu P. It's not

01:08:53.990 --> 01:08:56.609
like mu P is necessary for
you to train a good model.

01:08:56.609 --> 01:08:58.040
It's really just
an attempt to try

01:08:58.040 --> 01:09:00.300
to keep this shift
as small as possible,

01:09:00.300 --> 01:09:04.040
so you can use the same learning
rate throughout scaling.

01:09:04.040 --> 01:09:09.439
And if you go back to DeepSeek,
if you remember the scaling

01:09:09.439 --> 01:09:12.830
law that I was being
a slight hater for,

01:09:12.830 --> 01:09:15.410
you'll see that they
too have learning

01:09:15.410 --> 01:09:18.040
rates that go down as
a function of scale

01:09:18.040 --> 01:09:19.790
in order to try to
compensate for the fact

01:09:19.790 --> 01:09:22.300
that the bigger models are
going to have bigger updates.

01:09:22.300 --> 01:09:26.200
And so to respond more
directly to the question.

01:09:26.200 --> 01:09:29.890
Yes, in the case of DeepSeek,
as we scale the model up,

01:09:29.890 --> 01:09:31.865
our activation updates
will get bigger,

01:09:31.865 --> 01:09:33.740
so we have to shrink
the global learning rate

01:09:33.740 --> 01:09:35.573
or we should shrink the
global learning rate

01:09:35.573 --> 01:09:36.540
to compensate for that.

01:09:39.130 --> 01:09:39.640
Cool.

01:09:39.640 --> 01:09:41.200
OK.

01:09:41.200 --> 01:09:44.950
Nice questions.

01:09:44.950 --> 01:09:49.569
So that was the conceptual,
somewhat mathematical components

01:09:49.569 --> 01:09:55.270
of mu P. Now I want to talk
about the empirical aspects

01:09:55.270 --> 01:09:58.930
of mu P. And so I'm going
to talk through a preprint,

01:09:58.930 --> 01:10:00.580
or I think this one
may be published

01:10:00.580 --> 01:10:04.840
a column, a large scale
exploration of mu transfer.

01:10:04.840 --> 01:10:07.227
And I like this because it's
got a bunch of ablations.

01:10:07.227 --> 01:10:08.810
And I think I'm a
sucker for ablation,

01:10:08.810 --> 01:10:10.750
so I'll present any paper
that has large scale

01:10:10.750 --> 01:10:12.740
ablations in the course.

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:14.200
And so they do--

01:10:14.200 --> 01:10:16.735
essentially with mu P
as we've described it,

01:10:16.735 --> 01:10:18.110
just look at the
right hand side,

01:10:18.110 --> 01:10:20.560
which is the more relevant
piece, they're scaling down

01:10:20.560 --> 01:10:21.145
the variances.

01:10:21.145 --> 01:10:22.770
They're scaling down
the learning rates

01:10:22.770 --> 01:10:26.220
by the global width
of the models M.

01:10:26.220 --> 01:10:29.177
And they're primarily
keeping the depth fixed,

01:10:29.177 --> 01:10:31.260
which is a little bit of
an unusual scaling regime

01:10:31.260 --> 01:10:33.607
because usually you scale
depth and width together,

01:10:33.607 --> 01:10:35.940
but they really want to do a
controlled experiment where

01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:37.648
they're only looking
at width variations,

01:10:37.648 --> 01:10:39.480
and they want to
see if mu precisely

01:10:39.480 --> 01:10:43.170
nails scaling in this regime.

01:10:43.170 --> 01:10:46.980
There's also a little bit
of a weird subtlety that all

01:10:46.980 --> 01:10:50.850
of the mu P papers seem to do,
which is that if you remember

01:10:50.850 --> 01:10:54.720
your 224N lecture, you
remember that there's a scaling

01:10:54.720 --> 01:10:56.740
on the attention activations.

01:10:56.740 --> 01:11:00.300
You do your inner product, and
then you scale it down by 1

01:11:00.300 --> 01:11:02.310
over the square root
of D. And I told

01:11:02.310 --> 01:11:05.790
you this was a magic constant
that was the right thing to do.

01:11:05.790 --> 01:11:12.060
Mu P and other papers use 1
over D scaling instead of a 1

01:11:12.060 --> 01:11:15.000
over square root D for various
arguments related to activation

01:11:15.000 --> 01:11:17.293
and update size stability.

01:11:17.293 --> 01:11:18.960
And so that's another
thing that I think

01:11:18.960 --> 01:11:22.030
was worth pointing out because
you might not initially

01:11:22.030 --> 01:11:27.850
think of that as being something
that's related to mu P. OK.

01:11:27.850 --> 01:11:31.360
Architecture is mostly similar
to the standard transformer

01:11:31.360 --> 01:11:32.200
stuff.

01:11:32.200 --> 01:11:33.860
And as I already
mentioned before,

01:11:33.860 --> 01:11:35.440
they only consider
width scaling.

01:11:35.440 --> 01:11:37.180
So they take a
standard transformer

01:11:37.180 --> 01:11:40.580
trained autoregressively
on pretraining text.

01:11:40.580 --> 01:11:43.360
And they want to basically
make the model wider and wider

01:11:43.360 --> 01:11:47.100
and wider on the MLPs and
the model residual stream

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:47.600
dimensions.

01:11:47.600 --> 01:11:49.130
They're going to make that
bigger and bigger and bigger.

01:11:49.130 --> 01:11:51.940
And what they want is for
the optimum learning rate

01:11:51.940 --> 01:11:54.650
to remain the same as
they scale the width up.

01:11:54.650 --> 01:11:58.330
And if it remains stable, then
that's the big victory for mu P.

01:11:58.330 --> 01:12:00.760
So the game is hopefully
clear to everybody.

01:12:00.760 --> 01:12:02.230
You just want to scale width.

01:12:02.230 --> 01:12:06.670
I want my learning rate that's
optimal to stay the same.

01:12:06.670 --> 01:12:11.650
So question number
one is, does it work?

01:12:11.650 --> 01:12:14.150
Well, the answer is yes.

01:12:14.150 --> 01:12:18.290
So we have different
width, 128, 512, 2048.

01:12:18.290 --> 01:12:21.300
We have different learning
rates across the columns.

01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:24.450
And the idealized
strategy here is

01:12:24.450 --> 01:12:26.740
we run a sweep of learning
rates at the small scale.

01:12:26.740 --> 01:12:29.190
I pick the smallest
scale, and I scale

01:12:29.190 --> 01:12:33.540
that up, and hopefully that base
learning rate remains optimal.

01:12:33.540 --> 01:12:36.600
And yeah, it seems
rates transfer

01:12:36.600 --> 01:12:40.950
very reliably across model sizes
if we're doing this somewhat

01:12:40.950 --> 01:12:44.310
precise width scaling.

01:12:44.310 --> 01:12:47.250
And so then I think you
start asking questions

01:12:47.250 --> 01:12:50.820
of all right, very similar
to the previous question that

01:12:50.820 --> 01:12:53.200
was just asked of OK,
when does mu P break?

01:12:53.200 --> 01:12:55.000
So you can ask that
question in theory,

01:12:55.000 --> 01:12:57.250
but you can also ask that
question in practice.

01:12:57.250 --> 01:13:00.960
So I'm just going to try
all modern variations

01:13:00.960 --> 01:13:02.680
to architectures that people do.

01:13:02.680 --> 01:13:06.090
And then I'm going to ask, does
this hyperparameter transfer

01:13:06.090 --> 01:13:08.890
thing continue to hold under
these variations or not.

01:13:08.890 --> 01:13:10.890
And the paper is quite
nice because they just

01:13:10.890 --> 01:13:12.400
go through a lot
of different stuff.

01:13:12.400 --> 01:13:13.780
They'll vary the activations.

01:13:13.780 --> 01:13:16.410
They'll vary the batch sizes,
the initializations, the RMS

01:13:16.410 --> 01:13:17.550
norm gains.

01:13:17.550 --> 01:13:19.990
They'll even use a
really exotic optimizers,

01:13:19.990 --> 01:13:22.190
like sine gradient style stuff.

01:13:22.190 --> 01:13:24.555
And then they'll also
vary the regularizers.

01:13:24.555 --> 01:13:28.360
Which one of these prevents
learning rate transfer?

01:13:28.360 --> 01:13:31.270
So the first one, which I
think is probably relevant

01:13:31.270 --> 01:13:33.980
if you were m of looking at that
deep linear network and saying,

01:13:33.980 --> 01:13:36.590
oh, no one just multiplies
matrices together,

01:13:36.590 --> 01:13:38.290
there's nonlinearities
in between.

01:13:38.290 --> 01:13:41.390
So mu P work when we change
nonlinearities around.

01:13:41.390 --> 01:13:46.990
Well, SwiGLU, squared
ReLU, and the baseline mu

01:13:46.990 --> 01:13:51.890
P approach of ReLU, all have
the same minimal learning rate.

01:13:51.890 --> 01:13:53.740
So no changes at all.

01:13:53.740 --> 01:13:56.530
We just see that, for example,
SwiGLU and squared ReLU

01:13:56.530 --> 01:13:59.140
just do better than baseline 8.

01:13:59.140 --> 01:14:01.510
Unsurprising agrees
with a lot of what

01:14:01.510 --> 01:14:04.450
we've learned in the course.

01:14:04.450 --> 01:14:06.310
We might vary the
batch sizes, because we

01:14:06.310 --> 01:14:10.430
know that batch sizes are
going to be sensitive to scale,

01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:12.340
like we've seen
many CPM and we've

01:14:12.340 --> 01:14:15.370
seen DeepSeek basically fit
scaling loss to batch sizes

01:14:15.370 --> 01:14:18.340
to try to get what the
optimum batch size was.

01:14:18.340 --> 01:14:22.000
Once again, we see
that, as we scale up

01:14:22.000 --> 01:14:26.800
batch sizes by 4, up or
down, optimum learning rates

01:14:26.800 --> 01:14:29.020
remain stable.

01:14:29.020 --> 01:14:31.955
What about initializations?

01:14:31.955 --> 01:14:35.420
There are some initializations
that people vary.

01:14:35.420 --> 01:14:38.292
For example, some people
set the query matrix to 0

01:14:38.292 --> 01:14:40.750
so that all the different items
get uniform attention maybe

01:14:40.750 --> 01:14:42.340
that's more stable.

01:14:42.340 --> 01:14:46.220
Some people, the embedding
layer at the very top,

01:14:46.220 --> 01:14:48.670
they'll scale differently
based on either use standard

01:14:48.670 --> 01:14:52.000
parameterization or mu P.
Maybe that matters a lot.

01:14:52.000 --> 01:14:54.915
Turns out neither of those
do the center column.

01:14:54.915 --> 01:14:56.290
The optimum learning
rate remains

01:14:56.290 --> 01:14:59.890
optimal in all of these cases.

01:14:59.890 --> 01:15:01.640
What is it not robust to?

01:15:01.640 --> 01:15:05.260
Well, it's not going to
work for every single case.

01:15:05.260 --> 01:15:10.330
For example, if you
add learnable gains,

01:15:10.330 --> 01:15:14.020
that turns out to break mu P, so
you need to remove the biases.

01:15:14.020 --> 01:15:17.060
But if you remove
them, the mu P works.

01:15:17.060 --> 01:15:21.460
If you add them back in,
don't necessarily work.

01:15:21.460 --> 01:15:25.670
Similarly, you can try
more exotic optimizers.

01:15:25.670 --> 01:15:28.840
Lion is an optimizer
that takes like the sine

01:15:28.840 --> 01:15:31.870
of the gradient updates, which
to me feel a little bit crazy,

01:15:31.870 --> 01:15:34.120
but I think this was searched--
this was found through

01:15:34.120 --> 01:15:36.250
like evolutionary
search or something

01:15:36.250 --> 01:15:39.490
like this to find the
fastest optimizer.

01:15:39.490 --> 01:15:41.750
If you use this more
crazy optimizer,

01:15:41.750 --> 01:15:43.270
it really breaks down.

01:15:43.270 --> 01:15:45.190
And I think this
is what you expect.

01:15:45.190 --> 01:15:48.550
Mu P is designed to adapt to
a very particular optimizer

01:15:48.550 --> 01:15:51.410
like AdamW to control
the update sizes.

01:15:51.410 --> 01:15:53.968
So if you're using a
totally different optimizer,

01:15:53.968 --> 01:15:56.510
I don't know why you'd expect
the learning rates to transfer.

01:15:56.510 --> 01:16:00.850
So maybe expected
that this thing fails.

01:16:00.850 --> 01:16:03.370
And then finally, what is it--

01:16:03.370 --> 01:16:05.440
also not robust, it
turns out if you really

01:16:05.440 --> 01:16:10.150
have much stronger weight decay,
mu P actually starts to fail.

01:16:10.150 --> 01:16:13.190
And so this is one of the
few significant mu P failures

01:16:13.190 --> 01:16:13.940
that are in there.

01:16:13.940 --> 01:16:16.607
A lot of the other ones are just
like oh, we maybe expected that

01:16:16.607 --> 01:16:18.750
or that's not standard to do.

01:16:18.750 --> 01:16:21.250
Weight decay is something
that you actually do.

01:16:21.250 --> 01:16:22.290
OK.

01:16:22.290 --> 01:16:25.630
So mu P seems generally useful.

01:16:25.630 --> 01:16:27.867
If you take standard
parameterization going back

01:16:27.867 --> 01:16:30.450
to the baseline, you might ask
like, all right, what if I just

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:32.830
do standard baseline stuff.

01:16:32.830 --> 01:16:34.830
You can't use the
same learning rate.

01:16:34.830 --> 01:16:37.950
The same learning rate results
in significantly worse losses

01:16:37.950 --> 01:16:38.890
at 2048.

01:16:38.890 --> 01:16:41.400
Your model just blows
up, gives you basically,

01:16:41.400 --> 01:16:42.500
degenerate losses.

01:16:42.500 --> 01:16:45.000
You would have been very sad
scaling up at the same learning

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:45.637
rate.

01:16:45.637 --> 01:16:47.220
And we see also that
the learning rate

01:16:47.220 --> 01:16:51.390
needs to scale down predictably
as a function of the width.

01:16:51.390 --> 01:16:54.270
On the other hand, even if you
scale up all the way to a 10B

01:16:54.270 --> 01:16:57.690
parameter model, you see that
the base loss remains the same.

01:16:57.690 --> 01:16:59.710
So they do one large
scale experiment,

01:16:59.710 --> 01:17:02.820
and they see that the learning
rate remains ideal at the 2

01:17:02.820 --> 01:17:05.680
to the negative 6 level,
which is a cool validation.

01:17:05.680 --> 01:17:08.530
So they do the whole study
at a medium to small scale.

01:17:08.530 --> 01:17:12.060
They do one big hero run,
and then the learning rate

01:17:12.060 --> 01:17:13.670
remains optimal.

01:17:13.670 --> 01:17:17.140
So the empirical results on
that look somewhat promising.

01:17:17.140 --> 01:17:21.160
The fact that Meta used it for
Llama 4 is also quite nice.

01:17:21.160 --> 01:17:22.990
But as far as I know,
it's not a consensus

01:17:22.990 --> 01:17:26.200
that people use mu P. OK.

01:17:26.200 --> 01:17:30.760
So putting it all together,
how do you scale in the wild?

01:17:30.760 --> 01:17:36.225
I have never trained a 70B
model at super Chinchilla sizes.

01:17:36.225 --> 01:17:38.600
And so we're going to have to
rely a lot on case studies.

01:17:38.600 --> 01:17:42.410
And we saw several examples
of scaling in the wild.

01:17:42.410 --> 01:17:45.880
We saw people setting things
like model hyperparameters,

01:17:45.880 --> 01:17:49.850
especially learning rate and
batch sizes, using scaling laws.

01:17:49.850 --> 01:17:52.120
We saw people using
things like mu P

01:17:52.120 --> 01:17:56.320
or assume stability to try to
avoid search over these spaces.

01:17:56.320 --> 01:17:59.170
And then also, the use of
things like alternative learning

01:17:59.170 --> 01:18:02.890
schedules like WSD can
decrease the amount of compute

01:18:02.890 --> 01:18:05.630
that you need in order to fit
a lot of these scaling laws.

01:18:05.630 --> 01:18:08.910
So that's all I got.
